// Evidence types help content for assessment questions
// Structured as: { "QUESTION-CODE": "evidence help text" }

export const helpContent = {
  "AC-1": "To comply with **AC-1 (\"How is accountability for AI system failures\nmanaged within your organization?\")**, organizations must show that\n**clear ownership, structured processes, and governance mechanisms are\nin place to manage AI failures effectively.**\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Frameworks:** A formal *Accountability Policy* and\n  *Incident Management Framework* defining how AI failures are\n  identified, escalated, and resolved, with specific accountability\n  assignments.\n- **Roles & Responsibilities:** Documented role descriptions for AI\n  System Owners, Risk Managers, and Incident Response Teams, including\n  clear escalation paths and decision-making authority.\n- **Incident Management:** Evidence of *incident response plans*,\n  *incident logs*, and *post-incident analyses* outlining the nature,\n  impact, and resolution of failures.\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Minutes or approvals from ethics or\n  oversight committees verifying accountability processes are regularly\n  reviewed.\n- **Monitoring & Reporting:** Systems to detect failures, track\n  performance, and report incidents internally and externally.\n- **Stakeholder Engagement:** Communication protocols and feedback\n  mechanisms ensuring transparency with affected users or regulators.\n- **Training & Awareness:** Employee training on incident response and\n  accountability expectations.\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Lessons learned, root-cause analyses, and\n  updates to accountability policies.\n**In short:** Compliance requires demonstrable ownership, structured\nresponse processes, and transparent reporting to ensure responsible\nmanagement of AI failures.\n---",
  "AC-2": "To comply with **AC-2 (\"What governance structures are in place to\nmonitor AI outcomes?\")**, organizations must demonstrate that **formal\noversight mechanisms and governance frameworks** exist to monitor,\nreview, and guide AI system outcomes across ethical, legal, and\noperational dimensions.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Governance Framework:** A documented *AI Governance Framework*\n  outlining roles, responsibilities, and authority of governance\n  structures (e.g., AI Oversight Board, Ethics Committee, Risk Team).\n- **Policies:** *Monitoring and Reporting Policies* specifying how AI\n  performance, fairness, and compliance are tracked and reviewed.\n- **Governance Structures:** Organizational charts, committee charters,\n  and meeting records showing regular oversight of AI systems.\n- **Decision-Making Processes:** Minutes and approval logs from\n  governance body meetings evidencing active review and intervention.\n- **Oversight Mechanisms:** Use of dashboards, audits, or monitoring\n  tools to assess AI performance and detect anomalies.\n- **Risk Management:** Risk registers and mitigation plans maintained\n  and reviewed by governance teams.\n- **Accountability & Reporting:** Annual reports, post-incident\n  oversight, and escalation procedures.\n- **Regulatory & Ethical Compliance:** Proof of alignment with\n  frameworks such as GDPR and ISO/IEC AI governance standards.\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Evidence of periodic reviews, training,\n  and updates to governance processes.\n**In short:** Effective AI governance requires structured oversight,\ntransparent decision-making, and continuous monitoring to ensure safe,\nethical, and compliant AI outcomes.\n---",
  "AC-3": "To comply with **AC-3 (\"Are there policies for escalating issues\nidentified in the AI system?\")**, organizations must show that **formal\nescalation procedures exist** to ensure timely and appropriate handling\nof AI-related incidents, especially those with ethical, regulatory, or\noperational implications.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Escalation Policy:** A documented policy defining what issues\n  require escalation (e.g., critical failures, bias, security incidents)\n  and outlining how they progress through the organization.\n- **Roles & Responsibilities:** Clear role definitions and escalation\n  pathways involving AI System Owners, Risk Managers, and Incident\n  Response Leads.\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Records showing oversight bodies (e.g., AI\n  Governance Boards, Ethics Committees) review escalated issues and\n  approve resolutions.\n- **Detection & Reporting:** Use of monitoring tools and issue reporting\n  mechanisms (e.g., ticketing systems, anonymous reporting).\n- **Escalation Triggers:** Defined severity levels and thresholds\n  determining when and how issues are escalated.\n- **Communication Protocols:** Notification plans for internal\n  stakeholders, executives, and regulators.\n- **Training & Drills:** Evidence of employee training and simulations\n  to test escalation readiness.\n- **Continuous Review:** Logs, feedback loops, and audits verifying\n  escalation effectiveness and regulatory compliance.\n**In short:** Compliance requires structured, documented escalation\npolicies ensuring critical AI issues are identified, communicated, and\nresolved efficiently with full governance oversight.\n---",
  "AC-4": "To comply with **AC-4 (\"How do you handle customer complaints or\ndisputes related to AI outputs?\")**, organizations must show that\n**clear, fair, and transparent complaint-handling processes** exist to\naddress customer disputes about AI decisions.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Procedures:** A formal *Complaint Handling Policy* and\n  *Dispute Resolution Procedure* detailing how AI-related complaints are\n  received, investigated, resolved, and communicated.\n- **Accessible Channels:** Multiple customer-friendly options for\n  submitting complaints (e.g., online forms, helpdesk, chatbot, or\n  email) with multilingual and accessible design.\n- **Roles & Responsibilities:** Designated teams or officers (e.g., AI\n  Ethics Officer, Risk Manager, Customer Support) responsible for\n  reviewing and escalating complaints.\n- **Tracking & Analysis:** Complaint logs and systems (e.g., Zendesk,\n  Salesforce) recording issue details, timelines, root-cause analysis,\n  and resolution outcomes.\n- **Governance Oversight:** Ethics or AI oversight committees reviewing\n  recurring or high-impact disputes.\n- **Transparency:** Evidence of communications with customers confirming\n  receipt, updates, and outcomes of complaints.\n- **Regulatory & Ethical Compliance:** Adherence to consumer protection\n  laws, GDPR, and ethical principles like fairness and\n  non-discrimination.\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Trend reports, lessons learned, and\n  updates to procedures based on feedback.\n**In short:** Compliance requires documented, transparent, and inclusive\ncomplaint-handling processes ensuring fairness, accountability, and\ncustomer trust in AI outcomes.\n---",
  "AC-5": "To comply with **AC-5 (\"Are roles and responsibilities for AI governance\nclearly defined across teams?\")**, organizations must demonstrate that\n**accountability for AI governance is formalized, documented, and\nwell-communicated** across all relevant departments.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Governance Framework:** A documented AI Governance Framework\n  outlining roles and responsibilities across technical, legal, risk,\n  and compliance teams.\n- **Role Descriptions:** Clearly defined job or team descriptions\n  specifying accountability for AI design, deployment, monitoring, and\n  compliance oversight.\n- **Organizational Structure:** Organizational charts and committee\n  records (e.g., AI Ethics Board, Risk Committee) showing reporting\n  lines and governance ownership.\n- **Accountability Matrix:** Use of a RACI or similar tool identifying\n  who is *Responsible, Accountable, Consulted,* and *Informed* for each\n  governance activity.\n- **Communication Protocols:** Evidence of structured inter-team\n  communication (meeting records, collaboration procedures).\n- **Training & Awareness:** Programs educating employees on their AI\n  governance roles and duties.\n- **Governance Oversight:** Periodic audits or reviews assessing role\n  clarity and effectiveness.\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Updates to frameworks or policies\n  reflecting lessons learned and evolving regulations.\n**In short:** Compliance requires a **clear, organization-wide\ndefinition and communication of AI governance roles**---supported by\npolicies, documentation, training, and regular reviews---to ensure\nconsistent accountability and effective oversight.\n---",
  "AC-6": "To comply with **AC-6 (\"Who is responsible for monitoring and managing\nthe AI system throughout its lifecycle?\")**, organizations must\ndemonstrate that **clear ownership and structured processes exist for\ncontinuous oversight of AI systems** from design through\ndecommissioning.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Lifecycle Management Policy:** Formal documentation defining roles,\n  responsibilities, and procedures for monitoring, updating, and\n  retiring AI systems.\n- **Named Roles:** Clearly identified **AI System Owners**, **Monitoring\n  Leads**, and **Incident Response Teams**, supported by RACI matrices\n  assigning accountability across lifecycle stages.\n- **Governance Oversight:** Evidence of AI Ethics Boards or Risk\n  Committees periodically reviewing system performance, risks, and\n  compliance status.\n- **Monitoring & Maintenance:** Use of dashboards and automated tools to\n  track performance metrics, model drift, bias, or anomalies; regular\n  maintenance and patching schedules.\n- **Incident Management:** Defined escalation and resolution workflows\n  with detailed incident logs.\n- **Training & Resources:** Role-specific training and guides for staff\n  managing monitoring and compliance activities.\n- **Reporting & Feedback:** Established reporting lines to senior\n  management and feedback loops for ongoing improvement.\n- **Compliance & Audits:** Documentation and audit findings\n  demonstrating adherence to lifecycle monitoring requirements (e.g.,\n  ISO/IEC AI standards).\n**In short:** Compliance requires a **formal, role-based framework\nensuring continuous, accountable management** of AI systems throughout\ntheir entire operational lifecycle.\n---",
  "AC-7": "To comply with **AC-7 (\"Are there audit trails or logs that document the\nAI system's decision-making process?\")**, organizations must show that\n**comprehensive, secure, and reviewable logging mechanisms** exist to\nrecord how AI decisions are made and maintained over time.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Logging Policy:** A formal policy detailing how decision-making data\n  (inputs, model logic, outputs, timestamps) is logged, secured, and\n  retained.\n- **Technical Logs:** System-generated logs capturing input data,\n  decision steps, model parameters, and output results for traceability.\n- **Governance Oversight:** Regular reviews of logs by AI governance or\n  risk teams to ensure accountability and compliance.\n- **Integrity & Access Controls:** Role-based permissions, audit logs of\n  access activity, and integrity checks to prevent tampering.\n- **Incident Response:** Examples showing logs used to investigate and\n  resolve issues, validate model outcomes, or respond to\n  customer/regulator inquiries.\n- **Regulatory & Ethical Compliance:** Evidence of adherence to GDPR and\n  ISO/IEC standards on traceability and automated decision transparency.\n- **Tools & Systems:** Use of logging frameworks (e.g., Splunk, ELK\n  Stack) and integration with explainability tools like SHAP or LIME for\n  enhanced interpretability.\n- **Training & Reporting:** Staff trained on interpreting logs; periodic\n  reports generated from logged data for management and compliance.\n**In short:** Compliance requires **secure, traceable audit trails**\nthat make every AI decision explainable, verifiable, and accountable.\n---",
  "AC-8": "To comply with **AC-8 (\"What mechanisms are in place to address disputes\nor concerns about the system's outcomes?\")**, organizations must show\nthat **clear, fair, and accessible processes exist for users and\nstakeholders to raise, investigate, and resolve concerns** about\nAI-generated decisions.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Dispute Resolution Policy:** A formal policy defining the types of\n  disputes covered (e.g., incorrect results, bias, ethical concerns) and\n  how they are escalated.\n- **Accessible Channels:** User-friendly submission options such as web\n  forms, helpdesk tickets, or dedicated emails/chatbots, with options\n  for anonymity.\n- **Roles & Accountability:** Clearly defined responsibilities (e.g., AI\n  Ethics Officers, System Owners) supported by a RACI matrix and\n  oversight by ethics or risk committees.\n- **Investigation & Analysis:** Logs and root-cause reports showing how\n  disputes were reviewed, including bias and fairness assessments using\n  explainability tools like SHAP or LIME.\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Evidence of regular dispute reviews by\n  oversight boards and corrective action tracking.\n- **Reporting & Feedback:** Metrics on dispute volume, types, and\n  resolution times; stakeholder feedback loops.\n- **Training & Awareness:** Staff trained in dispute handling, fairness,\n  and stakeholder communication.\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Policy updates and lessons-learned reports\n  feeding back into AI system and governance enhancements.\n**In short:** Compliance requires **a transparent, accessible, and\nwell-governed dispute resolution framework** that builds trust and\nensures fairness in AI outcomes.\n---",
  "DI-1": "To comply with **DI-1 (\"How do you validate the accuracy, completeness,\nand quality of the data used in your AI systems?\")**, organizations must\nshow that **data integrity is actively monitored and validated**\nthroughout the AI lifecycle to ensure reliable, bias-free, and compliant\noutcomes.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Data Quality Policy:** Formal documentation defining accuracy,\n  completeness, and quality metrics (e.g., error rates, duplication\n  rates) and validation procedures.\n- **Validation Framework:** A structured approach combining automated\n  checks and human review to assess data integrity at collection,\n  preprocessing, and modeling stages.\n- **Validation Processes:** Reports or logs from completeness, accuracy,\n  and consistency tests using tools like *Great Expectations*, *Talend*,\n  or *OpenRefine*.\n- **Data Governance:** Clearly defined roles (data stewards, governance\n  committees) and systems for **data lineage tracking** and stewardship\n  accountability.\n- **Monitoring & Dashboards:** Real-time data quality dashboards\n  tracking trends, anomalies, and remediation actions.\n- **Audit & Review:** Internal and third-party audit findings verifying\n  adherence to data quality standards (e.g., ISO/IEC 27001, GDPR).\n- **Training & Awareness:** Staff training in data validation techniques\n  and ethical handling.\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Root-cause analyses and process updates to\n  correct recurring issues.\n**In short:** Compliance requires a **systematic, auditable data\nvalidation program** ensuring all AI inputs remain accurate, complete,\nand trustworthy.\n---",
  "DI-2": "To comply with **DI-2 (\"What processes do you have for managing data\nlineage and tracking changes to data over time?\")**, organizations must\nshow that **data origins, transformations, and changes are continuously\ntracked, documented, and governed** to ensure transparency,\ntraceability, and regulatory compliance.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Data Lineage Policy:** Formal policy defining how data sources,\n  transformations, and destinations are identified and recorded,\n  including scope, terminology, and update frequency.\n- **Change Management Policy:** Documented processes for recording,\n  approving, and version-controlling changes to data, schemas, or\n  pipelines.\n- **Lineage Tools:** Use of automated lineage and cataloguing tools\n  (*Collibra*, *Informatica*, *Alation*, *Talend*) or dataset version\n  control (*Git*, *DVC*) to visualize and trace data flow.\n- **Governance & Stewardship:** Assigned data stewards responsible for\n  maintaining lineage accuracy and monitoring data modifications.\n- **Documentation & Metadata:** Lineage diagrams, transformation logs,\n  and metadata records detailing data origins, movements, and changes.\n- **Change & Audit Logs:** Version-controlled logs recording what was\n  changed, when, by whom, and why---supported by monitoring dashboards\n  and integrity reports.\n- **Compliance Evidence:** Alignment with GDPR, SOX, ISO/IEC, or\n  sector-specific traceability standards.\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Regular reviews, root-cause analyses, and\n  updates to lineage policies and tools based on audit findings.\n**In short:** Compliance requires **end-to-end data traceability and\nchange control** across the entire AI data lifecycle.\n---",
  "DI-3": "To comply with **DI-3 (\"Are there governance policies for data\ncollection, usage, retention, and disposal?\")**, organizations must\ndemonstrate that **data is ethically, legally, and securely managed\nacross its entire lifecycle** --- from acquisition to destruction.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Comprehensive Data Governance Policy:** A formal policy outlining\n  procedures for **data collection, usage, retention, and disposal**,\n  incorporating regulatory and ethical requirements.\n  - **Collection:** Defines lawful and minimal data gathering, consent\n    mechanisms, and verification of data sources.\n  - **Usage:** Establishes approved purposes, access controls, and audit\n    trails to prevent misuse.\n  - **Retention:** Specifies justified retention periods aligned with\n    business and legal requirements, with periodic reviews.\n  - **Disposal:** Details secure deletion, destruction, or anonymization\n    processes, with disposal logs and third-party validation where\n    applicable.\n- **Compliance Alignment:** Evidence of adherence to frameworks like\n  **GDPR**, **Australian Privacy Act**, **HIPAA**, or **PCI DSS**, and\n  ethical principles such as fairness and privacy.\n- **Governance Roles:** Appointment of data protection officers or\n  stewards accountable for enforcing lifecycle compliance.\n- **Monitoring, Training, and Auditing:** Systems for continuous policy\n  monitoring, staff awareness programs, and regular internal/external\n  audits.\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Updates to governance policies based on\n  audit results, incidents, or regulatory changes.\n**In short:** Compliance requires **documented, enforceable lifecycle\npolicies** ensuring lawful, ethical, and secure data management.\n---",
  "DI-4": "To comply with **DI-4 (\"How do you ensure the data used in your AI\nsystems is up-to-date and accurate?\")**, organizations must show that\n**data freshness and accuracy are proactively maintained** through\nautomated, procedural, and governance-based controls.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Data Accuracy & Update Policy:** Formal documentation outlining\n  verification steps, update schedules, quality thresholds, and approval\n  workflows for data changes.\n- **Validation & Verification:** Regular data quality checks (e.g.,\n  accuracy, consistency, anomaly detection) and third-party verification\n  against trusted sources.\n- **Update Mechanisms:** Automated data refresh systems (APIs, cron\n  jobs, or streaming pipelines) and manual update procedures with review\n  checkpoints.\n- **Roles & Responsibilities:** Designated data stewards or owners\n  accountable for accuracy, update frequency, and issue escalation.\n- **Monitoring & Reporting:** Real-time dashboards and alerts for stale\n  or inaccurate data, along with periodic data quality performance\n  reports.\n- **Testing & Impact Analysis:** Logs showing pre-deployment validation\n  of updated datasets and reports evaluating their effect on AI model\n  outputs.\n- **Audit & Continuous Improvement:** Regular audits, root-cause\n  analyses, and policy refinements following data quality incidents.\n- **Compliance & Ethics:** Evidence of adherence to data quality\n  standards under frameworks like **GDPR**, **ISO/IEC**, or **Australian\n  Privacy Act**.\n**In short:** Compliance requires **continuous data validation,\nautomation, and accountability** to ensure AI decisions remain reliable\nand current.\n---",
  "DI-5": "To comply with **DI-5 (\"What processes are in place to address missing\nor incomplete data?\")**, organizations must demonstrate **systematic,\ndocumented methods for detecting, resolving, and preventing incomplete\ndatasets** that could impair AI accuracy or fairness.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Data Quality Policy:** Formal guidance on identifying and resolving\n  missing data, including defined completeness thresholds and approved\n  remediation techniques.\n- **Validation & Monitoring:** Automated checks (e.g., null value\n  detection, completeness scoring) and dashboards that track and\n  visualize data coverage gaps.\n- **Remediation Processes:**\n  - **Imputation:** Applying statistical (mean/median/mode), predictive,\n    or time-series imputation methods.\n  - **Collection Enhancements:** Improving input validation, form\n    design, or data entry controls to prevent gaps.\n  - **Manual Reviews:** Documented correction of missing data with\n    change tracking.\n- **Governance & Roles:** Designated data stewards or quality teams\n  accountable for monitoring completeness and resolving issues.\n- **Reporting & Auditing:** Regular completeness reports, incident logs,\n  and audits verifying adherence to completeness standards.\n- **Testing & Impact Analysis:** Evidence showing how missing data\n  affects model performance and steps taken to mitigate bias or\n  inaccuracy.\n- **Tools & Automation:** Integration of data cleaning and validation\n  tools (e.g., *Great Expectations*, *OpenRefine*, *TensorFlow Data\n  Validation*) into AI pipelines.\n**In short:** Compliance requires **proactive detection, remediation,\nand prevention** of missing data through policies, automation, and\ngovernance.\n---",
  "DI-6": "To comply with **DI-6 (\"How do you manage data preprocessing, e.g.,\ncleaning and normalization?\")**, organizations must demonstrate that\n**data is systematically cleaned, normalized, and transformed** before\nbeing used in AI models to ensure accuracy, consistency, and\nreliability.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Data Preprocessing Policy:** A formal policy and SOPs detailing\n  standardized methods for cleaning (duplicate removal, outlier\n  handling), normalization (scaling, encoding), and transformation\n  (aggregation, feature engineering).\n- **Tools & Automation:** Use of reliable preprocessing tools (e.g.,\n  *Pandas*, *NumPy*, *Scikit-learn*, *Trifacta*, *Talend*) and automated\n  pipelines within ML frameworks (e.g., *TensorFlow*, *PyTorch*) to\n  ensure repeatability and minimize human error.\n- **Quality Assurance:** Validation of preprocessed data through\n  statistical checks, automated quality reports, and preprocessing logs\n  documenting actions, rationales, and outcomes.\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Clear assignment of roles (data stewards,\n  engineers) responsible for preprocessing, with documented approval\n  processes and audit trails.\n- **Testing & Validation:** Reports showing how preprocessing enhances\n  model performance and mitigates bias, error, or drift.\n- **Compliance & Improvement:** Evidence that preprocessing adheres to\n  regulatory standards (e.g., GDPR, ISO/IEC 27001) and is periodically\n  reviewed for optimization.\n**In short:** Compliance requires **standardized, auditable, and\nwell-governed preprocessing workflows** that ensure clean, consistent,\nand reliable AI inputs.\n---",
  "DI-7": "To comply with **DI-7 (\"Are third-party data sources evaluated for\nquality and reliability?\")**, organizations must show they\n**systematically assess, validate, and monitor external data providers**\nto ensure the accuracy, integrity, and ethical use of third-party data.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Third-Party Data Policy:** Formal documentation defining evaluation\n  criteria such as data accuracy, completeness, timeliness, and provider\n  reliability.\n- **Vendor Due Diligence:** Records of quality and compliance\n  assessments of data providers, including audits of data collection\n  methods, certification checks (e.g., ISO, GDPR), and risk evaluations.\n- **Contracts & SLAs:** Agreements outlining data quality standards,\n  update frequencies, performance metrics, and responsibilities for\n  correction and security.\n- **Validation & Monitoring:** Regular testing of third-party data\n  against internal benchmarks, anomaly detection processes, and\n  dashboards tracking ongoing data quality.\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Defined approval frameworks for onboarding\n  providers, assigning accountability to procurement or data governance\n  teams.\n- **Audit & Issue Management:** Logs of periodic reviews, incidents, and\n  corrective actions tied to provider performance.\n- **Compliance & Ethics:** Documentation confirming adherence to legal\n  frameworks (e.g., GDPR, Australian Privacy Act) and ethical sourcing\n  standards.\n**In short:** Compliance requires a **formalized, auditable process for\nvetting, monitoring, and governing third-party data** to protect AI\nintegrity and stakeholder trust.\n---",
  "DI-8": "To comply with **DI-8 (\"How do you track data lineage to ensure\ntraceability across the AI lifecycle?\")**, organizations must\ndemonstrate that **all data flows---from source to AI output---are\ntraceable, documented, and auditable** to ensure transparency,\naccountability, and compliance.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Data Lineage Policy:** Formal policy defining the scope, processes,\n  and responsibilities for capturing and maintaining end-to-end lineage\n  across all AI lifecycle stages.\n- **Tools & Automation:** Use of lineage tracking systems (e.g.,\n  *Collibra*, *Alation*, *Informatica*, *Apache Atlas*, *MLflow*) to\n  automatically record metadata about data origins, transformations, and\n  destinations.\n- **Documentation & Visualization:** Lineage diagrams and metadata logs\n  showing how data is collected, processed, and used by specific models\n  or outputs.\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Appointed data stewards ensure lineage\n  accuracy and approve any workflow or source changes impacting\n  traceability.\n- **Audit & Monitoring:** Continuous monitoring dashboards, periodic\n  lineage validation, and internal/external audits confirming\n  completeness and integrity.\n- **Regulatory Compliance:** Alignment with GDPR accountability\n  principles, ISO/IEC 27001, and ethical AI transparency requirements.\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Regular updates to lineage tools,\n  processes, and policies based on feedback and lessons learned.\n**In short:** Compliance requires **automated, verifiable lineage\ntracking** that enables complete traceability of data through the entire\nAI lifecycle.\n---",
  "EX-1": "To comply with **EX-1 (\"How do you decide the level of explainability\nrequired for your AI system?\")**, organizations must show a\n**structured, risk-based process** for determining how much and what\nkind of explanation stakeholders need.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Criteria:** A formal *Explainability Policy* defining how\n  explainability levels are set, including decision factors such as\n  system complexity, regulatory demands, and ethical considerations.\n- **Risk & Impact Assessment:** Documentation showing evaluation of\n  potential harm, decision sensitivity (e.g., financial, medical), and\n  system scope to determine the depth of explanation needed.\n- **Stakeholder Analysis:** Records showing engagement with end-users,\n  regulators, and internal teams to identify their specific\n  explainability requirements.\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Evidence that explainability decisions are\n  reviewed and approved by AI oversight or ethics committees, with logs\n  documenting chosen explainability levels and rationale.\n- **Tools & Methodologies:** Use of frameworks or explainability tools\n  (e.g., *SHAP*, *LIME*) integrated during design, with documentation of\n  trade-offs between performance and interpretability.\n- **Monitoring & Review:** Records of periodic reassessments based on\n  regulatory changes or stakeholder feedback.\n**In short:** Compliance requires a documented, risk- and\nstakeholder-driven approach ensuring that AI explanations are\nproportionate, transparent, and aligned with ethical and regulatory\nexpectations.\n---",
  "EX-2": "To comply with **EX-2 (\"What methods are used to test whether\nexplanations are understandable by non-technical users?\")**,\norganizations must show that **AI explanations are tested for clarity,\naccessibility, and real-world comprehension** by diverse non-technical\naudiences.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Standards:** *Explainability Standards* and *User-Centric\n  Design Guidelines* defining clarity criteria and ensuring explanations\n  suit non-technical users.\n- **Testing & Evaluation:** Records of usability tests, pilot programs,\n  or focus groups measuring comprehension scores, satisfaction, and time\n  to understand explanations.\n- **Feedback Mechanisms:** Surveys or interviews capturing user feedback\n  on clarity, with results used to improve explanations iteratively.\n- **Visualization & Tools:** Use of visual aids (e.g., flowcharts,\n  dashboards) and explainability frameworks (*SHAP*, *LIME*,\n  counterfactuals) simplified for non-technical interpretation.\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Approval records and audit results\n  confirming explanations meet clarity and comprehension standards.\n- **Accessibility & Inclusion:** Language localization, assistive\n  features (e.g., screen reader compatibility), and inclusive testing\n  with varied user demographics.\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Feedback analysis, comprehension metrics,\n  and documented improvements based on testing outcomes.\n**In short:** Compliance requires structured user testing, stakeholder\nfeedback, and iterative refinements ensuring AI explanations are\ngenuinely understandable by non-technical users.\n---",
  "EX-3": "To comply with **EX-3 (\"Do you provide explanations in different formats\nfor different audiences?\")**, organizations must show that **AI\nexplanations are tailored to the needs, expertise, and preferences of\ndiverse stakeholders** through multiple, accessible formats.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Standards:** An *Explainability Policy* and\n  *Audience-Specific Standards* defining how explanations are adapted\n  for technical, non-technical, and regulatory audiences.\n- **Documentation & Resources:** Audience profiles or personas\n  identifying stakeholder needs, supported by repositories of\n  explanation templates (visual, textual, technical).\n- **Explanation Formats:**\n  - *Visualizations* (e.g., decision trees, graphs, interactive\n    dashboards).\n  - *Text Summaries* (plain-language overviews for non-technical users).\n  - *Technical Reports* (detailed algorithm documentation and metrics).\n  - *Multimedia* (videos, animations, tutorials).\n- **Stakeholder Testing & Feedback:** Usability studies, surveys, and\n  workshops validating clarity and effectiveness across audiences.\n- **Tools & Frameworks:** Use of *SHAP*, *LIME*, or visualization tools\n  like *Tableau* or *Power BI* to generate tailored outputs.\n- **Accessibility & Governance:** Multi-language support, accessibility\n  features, and governance reviews ensuring compliance with *GDPR* and\n  *ISO/IEC explainability standards*.\n**In short:** Compliance requires offering clear, inclusive, and\ncontext-appropriate AI explanations---so every audience can understand\nthe system's reasoning, regardless of expertise level.\n---",
  "EX-4": "To comply with **EX-4 (\"How do you evaluate the accuracy and\ncompleteness of the explanations provided?\")**, organizations must show\nthat **AI explanations are validated to accurately reflect system\nbehaviour and include all relevant decision factors.**\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Standards:** An *Evaluation Policy* defining how\n  explanation quality is assessed, with criteria covering accuracy,\n  completeness, and regulatory alignment.\n- **Testing & Validation:** Evidence of cross-checking explanations\n  against model logic, outputs, and input data; simulations ensuring\n  explanations remain correct across scenarios.\n- **Stakeholder Feedback:** Surveys, interviews, or feedback logs from\n  users, technical teams, and regulators confirming explanations are\n  understandable and accurate.\n- **Tools & Metrics:** Use of *SHAP*, *LIME*, or error analysis\n  frameworks to verify fidelity between model outputs and explanations;\n  performance metrics such as comprehension rates and fidelity scores.\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Review and approval records from\n  governance or ethics boards; audit reports verifying explanation\n  quality and completeness.\n- **Monitoring & Continuous Improvement:** Logs showing updates to\n  explanations as systems evolve or new issues arise.\n- **Compliance:** Evidence of adherence to *GDPR Article 22* and\n  *ISO/IEC explainability standards*.\n**In short:** Compliance requires a repeatable, measurable process\nensuring AI explanations are accurate, complete, and aligned with both\nsystem logic and stakeholder expectations.\n---",
  "EX-5": "To comply with **EX-5 (\"Are there trade-offs between model complexity\nand explainability in your system design?\")**, organizations must\ndemonstrate a **structured, transparent approach to balancing\nperformance with interpretability** throughout AI system development.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Standards:** *System Design Policies* and *Trade-Off\n  Guidelines* defining how explainability is prioritized---especially in\n  high-stakes or regulated applications.\n- **Design Documentation:** Trade-off analyses comparing complex (e.g.,\n  deep learning) vs. interpretable (e.g., decision tree) models, with\n  clear justifications for chosen architectures.\n- **Governance Oversight:** Evidence of governance or ethics committee\n  reviews approving trade-off decisions, with recorded rationales in\n  meeting logs.\n- **Testing & Evaluation:** Reports comparing performance gains from\n  complexity versus explainability reductions; user comprehension\n  testing of model outputs.\n- **Tools & Techniques:** Use of *SHAP*, *LIME*, or surrogate models to\n  enhance interpretability; adoption of hybrid model approaches for\n  balance.\n- **Stakeholder Engagement:** Records showing stakeholder consultation\n  and feedback on preferred trade-offs between accuracy and\n  transparency.\n- **Compliance & Ethics:** Adherence to *GDPR Article 22* and *ISO/IEC\n  23053* documentation standards for explainable AI.\n**In short:** Compliance requires evidence that model design decisions\ncarefully weigh performance against transparency, supported by\ngovernance review, stakeholder input, and ethical accountability.\n---",
  "EX-6": "To comply with **EX-6 (\"How do you ensure the decisions made by your AI\nsystem are interpretable by users?\")**, organizations must show that\n**AI decisions are designed, tested, and communicated in ways that users\ncan clearly understand and trust.**\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Standards:** An *Interpretability Policy* and\n  *Explainability Guidelines* defining acceptable interpretability\n  levels for different audiences (technical, non-technical, external).\n- **Design & Implementation:** Evidence that interpretability influenced\n  model selection (e.g., preference for decision trees or linear\n  models), supported by explainability tools (*SHAP*, *LIME*,\n  counterfactuals) and surrogate models for complex systems.\n- **Testing & Validation:** Usability tests, comprehension assessments,\n  and metrics showing users correctly understand AI decisions and\n  explanations.\n- **User Communication & Feedback:** Clear, user-facing\n  explanations---visual aids, text summaries, or dashboards---plus\n  feedback mechanisms driving continuous improvement.\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Review and approval of interpretability\n  measures by governance bodies, with periodic audits confirming\n  compliance.\n- **Compliance & Training:** Alignment with *GDPR* (right to\n  explanation) and *ISO/IEC 23053* standards, supported by staff\n  training on interpretability practices.\n**In short:** Compliance requires structured, user-tested, and\ntransparent design choices ensuring that AI decisions are both\ninterpretable and trustworthy for all stakeholders.\n---",
  "EX-7": "To comply with **EX-7 (\"What tools or techniques do you use to make\ncomplex models more explainable?\")**, organizations must show that\n**they use formal explainability tools---such as SHAP, LIME, or\nequivalents---to interpret and communicate how complex AI models make\ndecisions.**\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Tools & Techniques:** Documentation confirming the use of *SHAP*,\n  *LIME*, *Alibi*, *ELI5*, or custom visualization tools, including\n  rationale for their selection and application scope.\n- **Integration & Workflow:** Evidence that explainability tools are\n  built into the model development, validation, and deployment\n  pipelines, not just used post-hoc.\n- **Testing & Validation:** Reports verifying that explanation outputs\n  align with model behavior, alongside stakeholder testing for clarity\n  and accuracy.\n- **Training & Awareness:** Records showing staff training on\n  explainability tools and access to tool-specific guides or tutorials.\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Approvals from AI or data science\n  governance bodies reviewing the effectiveness of explainability tools.\n- **Stakeholder Communication:** Visual dashboards, feature importance\n  graphs, and plain-language summaries tailored for technical and\n  non-technical audiences.\n- **Compliance:** Alignment with *GDPR* and *ISO/IEC 23053*\n  explainability requirements.\n**In short:** Compliance requires consistent, validated, and transparent\nuse of recognized explainability tools to bridge the gap between complex\nmodels and human understanding.\n---",
  "EX-8": "To comply with **EX-8 (\"Are there specific groups for whom explanations\nof AI outputs are tailored?\")**, organizations must show that **AI\nexplanations are customized for distinct audiences**---such as\nend-users, regulators, and internal teams---based on their expertise,\nneeds, and roles.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Standards:** A formal *Explainability Policy* and\n  *Audience-Specific Standards* defining how explanations differ by\n  group (e.g., level of technical detail, regulatory depth).\n- **Stakeholder Analysis:** User profiles or personas documenting each\n  group's expectations, expertise, and preferred explanation formats.\n- **Tailored Mechanisms:** Concrete examples---plain-language\n  explanations or visuals for end-users, detailed compliance reports for\n  regulators, and technical documentation for internal teams.\n- **Testing & Feedback:** Usability testing, focus groups, or surveys\n  confirming explanations meet each audience's comprehension level and\n  purpose.\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Review and approval records verifying that\n  tailoring aligns with governance and regulatory requirements.\n- **Accessibility & Inclusivity:** Evidence of multi-language support,\n  accessibility features, and adaptations for diverse or disabled users.\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Feedback loops and metrics (e.g.,\n  comprehension or satisfaction scores) guiding iterative refinement.\n**In short:** Compliance requires structured, user-centred\nexplainability practices ensuring each stakeholder group receives clear,\nrelevant, and accessible AI explanations.\n---",
  "FA-1": "To demonstrate compliance with **FA-1** **(\"Identify and mitigate bias\nin AI systems\")**, look for evidence showing both **proactive** and\n**reactive** bias-mitigation measures across the AI lifecycle.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Governance:** A Bias Mitigation Policy or Fairness\n  Framework outlining responsibilities, oversight committees, and\n  escalation processes for bias incidents.\n- **Data & Model Practices:** Documentation proving datasets are diverse\n  and representative, preprocessing steps to rebalance or anonymize\n  data, and fairness testing or audits of models using tools such as\n  *Fairlearn*, *AI Fairness 360*, *SHAP*, or *LIME*.\n- **Training & Awareness:** Records of staff training or workshops on\n  bias awareness and ethical AI practices.\n- **Monitoring & Reporting:** Ongoing fairness monitoring, KPIs or\n  metrics (e.g., demographic parity, equal opportunity), and regular\n  internal or external reporting on bias management.\n- **Independent Validation:** Results of third-party bias audits or\n  fairness certifications.\n**In short:** Compliance requires documented processes, technical\nevidence of fairness testing, clear accountability, and continuous\nmonitoring. Gaps typically arise when bias detection is treated as a\none-off activity rather than an ongoing governance commitment.\n---",
  "FA-2": "To comply with **FA-2** **(\"Ensure training data represents all relevant\ndemographics\")**, look for evidence that training datasets are\n**diverse, representative, and regularly reviewed** for inclusivity.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Governance:** A documented *Data Representation Policy*\n  and *Diversity Standards* outlining minimum demographic inclusion\n  requirements, with oversight by a fairness or data governance\n  committee.\n- **Data Collection & Validation:** Proof that data is sourced from\n  multiple regions, demographics, or contexts; validation of historical\n  datasets for completeness and bias; and stakeholder or expert review\n  to confirm representativeness.\n- **Technical Controls:** Evidence of *data balancing*, *synthetic data\n  generation*, and use of bias-detection tools (e.g., *Fairlearn*, *AI\n  Fairness 360*). Metrics such as demographic parity or representation\n  indices should be used to measure fairness.\n- **Documentation & Audits:** Dataset composition reports showing\n  demographic breakdowns, versioning with metadata, and regular internal\n  or third-party audits assessing demographic inclusivity.\n- **Training & Awareness:** Records of staff education programs on\n  equitable data practices and awareness campaigns promoting fairness.\n**In short:** Compliance requires structured policies, measurable\nfairness criteria, and continuous monitoring to ensure training data\nfairly represents all groups the AI system affects.\n---",
  "FA-3": "To comply with **FA-3** **(\"Evaluate system outputs for potential\ndisparities across user groups\")**, look for **systematic evaluation,\ndocumentation, and ongoing monitoring** of fairness in AI outcomes.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Governance:** A formal *Evaluation or Fairness Policy*\n  defining how output disparities are identified, acceptable disparity\n  thresholds, and who is accountable for reviews.\n- **Tools & Metrics:** Use of fairness tools (e.g., *Fairlearn*, *AI\n  Fairness 360*, *What-If Tool*) and metrics such as *disparate impact*,\n  *demographic parity*, or *equalized odds* to measure disparities.\n- **Testing & Validation:** Evidence of scenario testing, demographic\n  segmentation, edge-case analysis, and documented reports highlighting\n  any detected disparities.\n- **Monitoring & Improvement:** Ongoing audits, real-time monitoring\n  dashboards, and feedback loops to retrain or adjust models when\n  disparities are found.\n- **Documentation & Oversight:** Records of disparity assessments,\n  corrective actions, and oversight by an AI fairness or ethics board.\n- **Independent Review:** Third-party audits or fairness certifications\n  validating results and compliance with standards (e.g., *ISO/IEC\n  24027*).\n**In short:** Compliance means consistently analysing and reporting AI\noutputs for unequal impacts, acting on findings, and maintaining\ntransparency and accountability in fairness evaluation processes.\n---",
  "FA-4": "To comply with **FA-4 (\"How do you measure fairness in your AI system's\noutputs?\")**, organizations must **define, quantify, and continuously\nmonitor fairness** using clear metrics and evidence-based evaluation.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Governance:** A *Fairness Measurement Policy* that\n  defines fairness objectives, metrics, and acceptable thresholds for\n  the organization's AI systems.\n- **Metrics & Evaluation:** Use of recognised fairness\n  metrics---*demographic parity*, *equalized odds*, *predictive parity*,\n  *disparate impact ratio*---and, where needed, context-specific custom\n  metrics aligned with ethical principles.\n- **Tools & Technology:** Implementation of fairness evaluation\n  platforms (e.g., *Fairlearn*, *AI Fairness 360*, *What-If Tool*,\n  *Responsible AI Toolbox*) and automated systems to track fairness in\n  real time.\n- **Testing & Reporting:** Documented fairness testing reports,\n  dashboards visualizing fairness scores, and periodic reviews covering\n  model updates, anomaly alerts, and corrective actions.\n- **Stakeholder & Compliance:** Evidence of fairness review boards,\n  staff training, stakeholder feedback, and alignment with *ISO/IEC\n  24027* or anti-discrimination regulations.\n**In short:** Compliance requires measurable fairness standards,\ntransparency in evaluation, and continuous monitoring to ensure outputs\nremain equitable, auditable, and aligned with ethical and regulatory\nexpectations.\n---",
  "FA-5": "To comply with **FA-5 (\"Have you tested the system for potential biases\nin real-world scenarios?\")**, look for **evidence of live environment\ntesting, stakeholder input, and bias correction processes** beyond lab\nconditions.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Governance:** A *Real-World Testing Policy* outlining how\n  and when systems are tested in operational contexts, with defined\n  fairness thresholds and approval processes before deployment.\n- **Testing Practices:** Documentation of *scenario-based testing*,\n  *pilot deployments*, and *A/B testing* across diverse demographics,\n  geographies, and edge cases to identify bias under real conditions.\n- **Results & Analysis:** Reports detailing real-world bias testing\n  methodologies, disparities observed, and metrics used (e.g.,\n  *demographic parity*, *equal opportunity*, *disparate impact ratio*).\n- **Tools & Adjustments:** Use of fairness and explainability tools\n  (e.g., *Fairlearn*, *AI Fairness 360*, *SHAP*, *LIME*) and evidence of\n  model updates, retraining logs, or changelogs addressing discovered\n  biases.\n- **Feedback & Oversight:** Input from users, experts, and governance\n  committees validating findings, with continuous monitoring and\n  periodic bias reports.\n- **Independent Review:** Third-party validation or certifications\n  confirming fairness testing integrity.\n**In short:** Compliance requires systematic real-world bias testing,\ntransparent reporting, and continuous refinement to ensure fairness\nholds beyond controlled development environments.\n---",
  "FA-6": "To comply with **FA-6 (\"Are you using any frameworks or tools to assess\nand mitigate bias?\")**, organizations should show **active, structured\nuse of recognized AI fairness frameworks** integrated into their\ndevelopment and governance processes.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Tools & Frameworks:** Proof of using fairness tools such as\n  *Fairlearn*, *IBM AI Fairness 360*, *Google's What-If Tool*, or\n  *Microsoft's Responsible AI Toolbox* to detect, measure, and mitigate\n  bias in datasets and models.\n- **Policies & Integration:** A *Bias Mitigation Policy* or *Fairness\n  Framework* mandating these tools as part of the AI lifecycle, with\n  documented inclusion in development workflows or automated pipelines.\n- **Reports & Outputs:** Tool-generated outputs showing detected\n  disparities, mitigation actions, and measurable fairness improvements\n  across demographic groups.\n- **Training & Governance:** Evidence that staff are trained to use\n  these tools and that fairness evaluations form a required approval\n  step before deployment.\n- **Continuous Monitoring:** Ongoing tool usage for real-time fairness\n  tracking, dashboards, and audit logs showing consistent bias\n  evaluation.\n- **External Validation:** Third-party audits or certifications\n  confirming compliance with standards such as *ISO/IEC 24027*.\n**In short:** Compliance requires demonstrable, ongoing use of trusted\nbias-assessment tools, embedded governance processes, and documented\nimprovements in fairness over time.\n---",
  "FA-7": "To comply with **FA-7 (\"How do you ensure fairness in decision-making\nfor minority or underserved groups?\")**, organizations must show\n**active measures to identify, prevent, and correct bias that could\ndisadvantage vulnerable populations.**\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Governance:** A *Fairness Policy* and *Ethics & Inclusion\n  Guidelines* explicitly protecting minority and underserved groups,\n  backed by oversight from a fairness review board or ethics committee.\n- **Data & Model Practices:** Documentation showing diverse and\n  representative datasets, bias audits, and use of techniques such as\n  *oversampling*, *synthetic data generation*, or *threshold\n  adjustments* to improve equity.\n- **Metrics & Tools:** Evidence of fairness evaluation using *disparate\n  impact ratio*, *equalized odds*, or *demographic parity*; and tools\n  such as *Fairlearn*, *AI Fairness 360*, *What-If Tool*, or *SHAP/LIME*\n  for explainability.\n- **Monitoring & Engagement:** Continuous monitoring for disparities,\n  incident reporting mechanisms, and stakeholder input from affected\n  communities.\n- **Training & Compliance:** Staff training on fairness principles,\n  adherence to anti-discrimination laws, and alignment with *ISO/IEC\n  24027* or ethical AI standards.\n**In short:** Compliance means embedding fairness for minority and\nunderserved groups throughout the AI lifecycle --- from dataset design\nand testing to governance, transparency, and ongoing accountability.\n---",
  "FA-8": "To comply with **FA-8 (\"Is fairness embedded in your AI model's design\nprocess, or is it addressed post-deployment?\")**, organizations must\nshow that **fairness is built in from the start**, not treated as a\nreactive fix.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Frameworks:** A *Fairness-by-Design Policy* and\n  *End-to-End Fairness Standards* embedding fairness considerations\n  throughout the AI lifecycle---from data collection to deployment and\n  monitoring.\n- **Design Integration:** Documentation of *inclusive design practices*,\n  *fairness checkpoints* during data preparation, model training, and\n  validation, and approval gates requiring fairness reviews before\n  progression.\n- **Data & Model Practices:** Evidence of representative datasets,\n  proactive bias detection, and application of bias mitigation\n  techniques (e.g., reweighting, adversarial debiasing).\n- **Tools & Oversight:** Use of fairness and explainability tools\n  (*Fairlearn*, *AI Fairness 360*, *Responsible AI Toolbox*, *SHAP*,\n  *LIME*) and oversight committees enforcing fairness compliance.\n- **Monitoring & Reporting:** Real-time fairness monitoring, incident\n  management logs, and documented audits or reports showing continuous\n  improvement.\n- **Training & Engagement:** Evidence of design team training, ethics\n  workshops, and stakeholder involvement in shaping fairness standards.\n**In short:** Compliance requires demonstrating a *fairness-by-design\napproach*---integrating equity, transparency, and accountability into\nevery phase of AI system development and beyond.\n---",
  "IN-1": "To comply with **IN-1 (\"How do you ensure your AI system is accessible\nto a diverse range of users, including those with disabilities or in\nunderserved populations?\")**, organizations must demonstrate a\n**commitment to inclusive, accessible AI design and operation**.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Procedures:** A formal **Accessibility Policy** and\n  **Inclusion & Diversity Policy** referencing standards like **WCAG\n  2.1**, **ADA**, **Section 508**, or **EN 301 549**, showing\n  organizational commitment to equitable access.\n- **User-Centric Design:** Inclusive design documentation with **diverse\n  personas**, accessibility considerations (e.g., screen reader support,\n  high-contrast modes, multilingual capability), and testing with\n  **diverse participants**.\n- **Accessibility Features:** Evidence of implemented features such as\n  **speech-to-text, keyboard navigation, voice interaction, and\n  adjustable UI elements**.\n- **Testing & Feedback:** Reports from **accessibility testing tools**\n  (e.g., Axe, WAVE, Lighthouse) and stakeholder feedback loops involving\n  advocacy groups and underserved communities.\n- **Training & Governance:** Staff training on inclusive design\n  principles and oversight by **inclusion committees** to ensure\n  continuous accessibility improvements.\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Regular audits, updates, and accessibility\n  performance tracking.\n**In short:** Compliance demonstrates that the AI system is **usable,\ninclusive, and accessible to all individuals**, ensuring equitable\nparticipation regardless of ability, language, or background.\n---",
  "IN-2": "To comply with **IN-2 (\"Were diverse perspectives considered during the\ndesign and development of the system?\")**, organizations must\ndemonstrate that **a variety of voices, experiences, and disciplines\ninformed the AI system's creation**.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Stakeholder Engagement:** Documentation showing consultations with\n  **diverse end-users**, DEI experts, advocacy groups, and\n  underrepresented communities. Structured feedback mechanisms that\n  ensure insights are incorporated into design decisions.\n- **Diverse Team Composition:** Reports showing **inclusive\n  representation** across gender, ethnicity, geography, and roles\n  (technical, ethical, social). Evidence that diverse team members had\n  **decision-making influence**.\n- **Inclusive Design Frameworks:** Adoption of principles such as\n  **Microsoft's Inclusive Design** or **Universal Design for Learning\n  (UDL)**, supported by diversity checklists and ethical impact\n  assessments.\n- **User Research & Testing:** Proof that user research and testing\n  included diverse participants, influencing usability and accessibility\n  features.\n- **Training & Governance:** DEI training for development teams and\n  oversight from **diversity review boards** or inclusion committees.\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Documentation showing ongoing refinement\n  based on feedback from marginalized or diverse groups.\n**In short:** Compliance demonstrates that **diversity was embedded from\ndesign to deployment**, ensuring the AI system serves broad, equitable,\nand inclusive user needs.\n---",
  "IN-3": "To comply with **IN-3 (\"Have you audited the system for potential\nexclusionary outcomes or unintended impacts?\")**, organizations must\nshow that **systematic audits are conducted to detect and correct bias\nor harm affecting specific groups**.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Procedures:** A formal **Audit Policy** defining the\n  scope, objectives, and methodology for inclusivity and fairness\n  audits, supported by an **Ethical AI Policy** committing to ongoing\n  impact assessment.\n- **Audit Records & Reports:** Completed **audit reports** identifying\n  exclusionary outcomes, demographic disparities, or unintended\n  social/economic impacts, including documented **mitigation plans**.\n- **Methodology & Tools:** Use of **established frameworks** (e.g., NIST\n  AI RMF, ISO 27501, IEEE 7000) and bias detection tools like\n  **Fairlearn** or **IBM AI Fairness 360**, alongside fairness metrics\n  (e.g., demographic parity, equalized odds).\n- **Data & Stakeholder Engagement:** **Dataset audits**, **disparity\n  analyses**, and **consultation records** showing engagement with\n  affected groups and external experts.\n- **Governance & Improvement:** Oversight by **audit committees** or\n  ethics boards, with **corrective actions**, **follow-up audits**, and\n  lessons learned integrated into future updates.\n**In short:** Compliance demonstrates that the organization **actively\nidentifies, measures, and mitigates bias or exclusion**, ensuring\nequitable outcomes and continuous fairness improvements in AI\noperations.\n---",
  "IN-4": "To comply with **IN-4 (\"How do you ensure your AI system accounts for\naccessibility requirements (e.g., for users with disabilities)?\")**,\norganizations must demonstrate a **structured and standards-aligned\napproach to accessibility** in design, testing, and maintenance.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Standards:** A documented **Accessibility Policy**\n  referencing **WCAG 2.1**, **ADA**, **EN 301 549**, or equivalent,\n  supported by an **Inclusivity Policy** embedding accessibility in AI\n  lifecycle governance.\n- **Accessibility Features:** Evidence of implemented features such as\n  **screen-reader compatibility, speech-to-text/text-to-speech, keyboard\n  navigation, high-contrast modes, scalable text**, and multi-modal\n  inputs (text, voice, visual).\n- **Testing & Validation:** Reports from **accessibility audits** (using\n  Axe, WAVE, Lighthouse, etc.) and **user testing** with people with\n  disabilities. Demonstrate **feedback loops** where issues were\n  identified and fixed.\n- **Design & Training:** Use of **Inclusive or Universal Design\n  frameworks** and training programs for developers to apply\n  accessibility best practices from the outset.\n- **Governance & Improvement:** Oversight by **accessibility review\n  committees**, integration with **assistive technologies** (JAWS, NVDA,\n  Braille displays), and **continuous improvement records** documenting\n  updates and lessons learned.\n**In short:** Compliance proves the AI system is **usable, equitable,\nand barrier-free**, ensuring full participation for users with diverse\nabilities.\n---",
  "IN-5": "To comply with **IN-5 (\"Are stakeholders from diverse backgrounds\ninvolved in the design and testing of the system?\")**, organizations\nmust show that **diversity and inclusion are actively embedded in\nstakeholder engagement throughout the AI lifecycle**.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Stakeholder Engagement:** Records of consultations, workshops, or\n  interviews with stakeholders from **different genders, ethnicities,\n  ages, and socioeconomic groups**, including marginalized or\n  underserved communities.\n- **Diverse Team Composition:** Documentation showing diverse project\n  teams across roles (technical, ethical, and social), supported by\n  **inclusive hiring and participation policies**.\n- **Feedback Mechanisms:** Logs capturing stakeholder input, observed\n  inclusivity gaps, and **evidence that feedback informed system\n  refinements**.\n- **Testing and Validation:** Reports showing **user testing and beta\n  trials** with demographically diverse participants, emphasizing\n  usability, fairness, and accessibility.\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Existence of **diversity review\n  committees** and oversight reports validating stakeholder inclusion\n  across project stages.\n- **Training & Frameworks:** Records of DEI training and use of\n  **inclusive design toolkits** or frameworks (e.g., Universal Design,\n  Microsoft Inclusive Design).\n**In short:** Compliance demonstrates that the organization **engaged a\nbroad spectrum of stakeholders** to ensure its AI system is equitable,\nrepresentative, and responsive to the needs of all user groups.\n---",
  "IN-6": "To comply with **IN-6 (\"What steps are taken to ensure that the AI\nsystem serves underrepresented communities effectively?\")**,\norganizations must show a **proactive and measurable commitment to\nequity, representation, and inclusion** in both data and design.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Commitments:** A documented **Inclusivity Policy** and\n  **Diversity and Equity Guidelines** that define how AI systems will\n  address the unique needs of marginalized or underserved groups.\n- **Data Representation:** **Data audits** and **diverse dataset\n  sourcing** to ensure demographic and socioeconomic representation,\n  along with bias mitigation or data augmentation where gaps exist.\n- **Community Engagement:** Records of **consultations, workshops, and\n  user feedback** involving underrepresented communities to inform\n  design decisions and system refinements.\n- **Inclusive Design & Testing:** Design documentation showing\n  **multilingual support, accessibility options, and adaptable\n  interfaces**, tested specifically with users from these communities.\n- **Monitoring & Governance:** Ongoing **performance metrics**,\n  **feedback loops**, and oversight by **inclusivity review committees**\n  or independent auditors.\n- **Training & Standards Alignment:** Staff training on equity in AI and\n  compliance with standards like **ISO 27501** and **IEEE 7010**.\n**In short:** Compliance demonstrates that the AI system is\n**intentionally designed, tested, and governed** to deliver equitable\nbenefits and minimize harm for underrepresented communities.\n---",
  "IN-7": "To comply with **IN-7 (\"Are language, cultural, or regional differences\nconsidered in system design?\")**, organizations must demonstrate that\n**localization and cultural sensitivity are built into every stage** of\nAI system development and deployment.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Guidelines:** A documented **Cultural Sensitivity\n  Policy** and **Localization Guidelines** detailing how language,\n  cultural, and regional factors are incorporated into system design and\n  functionality.\n- **Data Representation:** Diverse datasets representing multiple\n  **languages, cultures, and regions**, supported by **data audits** to\n  identify and address representation gaps or biases.\n- **Design & Features:** Evidence of **multilingual NLP capabilities**,\n  **localized interfaces**, **region-specific configurations** (dates,\n  currency, regulations), and **culturally adaptive content** that\n  avoids insensitivity or bias.\n- **Testing & Validation:** Reports from **localized and bias testing**\n  verifying that the system performs effectively across cultural and\n  regional contexts.\n- **Stakeholder Engagement:** Input from **linguists, cultural experts,\n  and regional users** to refine system design and outputs.\n- **Governance & Improvement:** Ongoing **monitoring, user feedback\n  loops, and audits** ensuring continuous enhancement of cultural\n  relevance and inclusivity.\n**In short:** Compliance proves the AI system is **linguistically\nadaptable, culturally aware, and regionally relevant**, minimizing\nexclusion and maximizing effectiveness across diverse user populations.\n---",
  "IN-8": "To comply with **IN-8 (\"How do you evaluate whether the system is\nunintentionally excluding certain user groups?\")**, organizations must\ndemonstrate that **structured, ongoing evaluations are conducted to\nidentify and correct exclusionary outcomes**.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Guidelines:** A formal **Inclusivity Assessment Policy**\n  defining how exclusion risks are identified, assessed, and mitigated\n  during the AI lifecycle.\n- **Data Representation:** **Dataset audits** ensuring demographic and\n  socioeconomic diversity, with documentation of actions taken to fix\n  underrepresentation or bias.\n- **Testing & Evaluation:** Reports from **bias, accessibility, and\n  real-world simulations** assessing how outputs vary across demographic\n  groups using fairness metrics (e.g., disparate impact ratios).\n- **User & Community Feedback:** Records of **feedback loops and\n  consultations** with diverse or underrepresented users and advocacy\n  groups to detect usability barriers.\n- **Monitoring & Analytics:** **Usage and performance metrics**\n  comparing engagement and outcomes across groups to flag disparities.\n- **Tools & Corrective Actions:** Use of **bias detection platforms**\n  (e.g., IBM AI Fairness 360, Fairlearn) and documented remediation\n  efforts with re-evaluations.\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Involvement of **inclusivity committees\n  and audits** ensuring regular exclusion assessments.\n**In short:** Compliance demonstrates the organization **actively\nmonitors, tests, and improves inclusivity**, ensuring no user group is\nunintentionally disadvantaged by the AI system.\n---",
  "PR-1": "To comply with **PR-1 (\"How do you ensure compliance with data privacy\nlaws such as GDPR or the Australian Privacy Act?\")**, organizations must\nshow **end-to-end data privacy governance** that aligns with legal,\ntechnical, and procedural obligations.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Procedures:** Published Privacy Policy, Data Protection\n  Policy, and Consent Management Policy outlining how personal data is\n  collected, processed, stored, and deleted.\n- **Data Mapping:** Comprehensive data inventories and Records of\n  Processing Activities (RoPA) identifying data flows, storage, and\n  retention timelines.\n- **Data Subject Rights:** Processes and logs demonstrating timely\n  handling of requests for access, correction, erasure, and portability.\n- **Legal Compliance:** Evidence of Privacy Impact Assessments (PIAs),\n  regulator alignment (GDPR, Australian Privacy Act), and vendor\n  compliance documentation.\n- **Consent Management:** Records and systems tracking user consent and\n  withdrawal; cookie consent tools.\n- **Protection Measures:** RBAC enforcement, encryption, anonymization,\n  pseudonymization, and data minimization.\n- **Governance:** Appointment of a Data Protection Officer (DPO) and\n  active privacy oversight committees.\n- **Monitoring & Auditing:** Regular audits, breach logs, and continuous\n  monitoring for compliance issues.\n- **Certifications:** ISO/IEC 27701, GDPR certifications, and\n  third-party audit reports.\n**In short:** Compliance demands **proactive privacy governance,\ndemonstrable consent and data-protection practices, and continuous\nalignment with evolving regulatory requirements.**\n---",
  "PR-2": "To comply with **PR-2 (\"What techniques do you use to protect personal\ndata in your AI workflows?\")**, organizations must demonstrate the use\nof **anonymization, pseudonymization, and other privacy-enhancing\ntechniques** that prevent re-identification while maintaining data\nutility.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Procedures:** Formal Data Protection and Privacy Policies\n  defining anonymization and pseudonymization processes and when each\n  method applies across the AI lifecycle.\n- **Techniques & Implementation:** Evidence of anonymization\n  (generalization, suppression, differential privacy) and\n  pseudonymization (tokenization, encryption, unique identifiers)\n  applied during data preprocessing.\n- **Data Management:** Data flow maps and processing records showing\n  where and how personal identifiers are transformed or removed.\n- **Tools & Technologies:** Use of PETs (e.g., OpenDP, Privitar,\n  Protegrity), k-anonymity algorithms, and secure key management via AWS\n  KMS or Azure Key Vault.\n- **Testing & Validation:** Regular re-identification risk assessments\n  and vulnerability tests to ensure transformations are irreversible and\n  effective.\n- **Access Control & Segmentation:** RBAC enforcement, separation of raw\n  and anonymized datasets, and encrypted key management.\n- **Governance & Oversight:** DPO supervision, internal audits, and\n  periodic policy updates reflecting evolving best practices.\n**In short:** Compliance requires **provable de-identification\nprocesses, tested privacy techniques, and continuous governance to\nprevent re-identification risks** in AI workflows.\n---",
  "PR-3": "To comply with **PR-3 (\"How do you manage user consent for data\ncollection and processing?\")**, organizations must show that they\n**obtain, record, and manage consent transparently** in line with\nprivacy laws like GDPR and the Australian Privacy Act.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Procedures:** Documented Consent Management Policy and\n  Privacy Policy detailing how consent is requested, stored, and\n  withdrawn.\n- **Consent Mechanisms:** Clear, jargon-free consent forms; granular\n  opt-in options; pop-ups or cookie banners compliant with privacy\n  regulations.\n- **Records & Audit Trails:** Logs capturing when and how consent was\n  obtained, what users agreed to, and the tools used (e.g., OneTrust,\n  TrustArc, Cookiebot).\n- **Withdrawal & Updates:** Easy interfaces for users to revoke consent;\n  records showing timely updates to data practices and renewed consent\n  requests.\n- **Transparency & Communication:** Layered privacy notices and\n  accessible statements clarifying purposes of data use.\n- **Monitoring & Reporting:** Systems ensuring data is not processed\n  without valid consent, with regular reviews and compliance dashboards.\n- **Governance & Training:** Staff training on consent management and\n  incident response plans for consent breaches.\n**In short:** Compliance requires **clear, trackable, and\nuser-controlled consent processes** supported by policies, automation\ntools, and continuous oversight.\n---",
  "PR-4": "To comply with **PR-4 (\"How do you ensure that personal data is not used\nbeyond its original purpose?\")**, organizations must demonstrate strict\n**purpose limitation controls** that prevent personal data from being\naccessed or repurposed outside its intended scope.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Procedures:** Data Usage Policy defining authorized\n  purposes and prohibiting secondary use; Privacy Policy publicly\n  stating data purposes.\n- **Consent & Documentation:** Consent logs confirming data use aligns\n  with user agreements and that consent specifies exact purposes.\n- **Data Governance:** Data classification and tagging systems labeling\n  data with permitted uses; lifecycle documentation tracking data from\n  collection to disposal.\n- **Access Controls:** RBAC restricting access to data only for\n  personnel and applications with a legitimate, consented purpose.\n- **Monitoring & Auditing:** Automated tools and alerts detecting\n  unauthorized access or usage beyond stated purposes; regular audits of\n  data flows.\n- **Minimization & Retention:** Collecting only essential data and\n  securely deleting or anonymizing it once its original purpose is\n  fulfilled.\n- **Training & Oversight:** Staff awareness programs, governance\n  committees, and PIAs verifying ongoing compliance.\n**In short:** Compliance relies on **clear purpose definition, strict\naccess and monitoring controls, and transparent lifecycle management**\nensuring data is used solely for its intended purpose.\n---",
  "PR-5": "To comply with **PR-5 (\"What methods do you use to anonymize or\npseudonymize personal data?\")**, organizations must demonstrate\n**technical and procedural safeguards** ensuring sensitive data cannot\nbe traced back to individuals while maintaining analytical utility for\nAI processing.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Procedures:** Data Protection and Privacy Policies\n  defining anonymization/pseudonymization methods, responsible roles,\n  and governance structures.\n- **Anonymization Techniques:** Use of **generalization**,\n  **suppression**, **differential privacy**, or **data aggregation** to\n  irreversibly remove identifiers; re-identification risk assessments\n  proving effectiveness.\n- **Pseudonymization Techniques:** Use of **tokenization**, **hashing\n  (with salt)**, and **encryption (e.g., AES-256)** to replace\n  identifiers with non-identifiable values, supported by secure key\n  management (AWS KMS, Azure Key Vault, HashiCorp Vault).\n- **Implementation in Workflows:** Application of de-identification\n  across all data lifecycle stages---collection, preprocessing, storage,\n  and archival---preferably automated to reduce human error.\n- **Governance & Oversight:** DPO review and audit logs confirming\n  anonymization steps and approvals.\n- **Regulatory Compliance:** Evidence aligning with **GDPR Article 25**,\n  **Australian Privacy Act**, and **ISO/IEC 27701** standards.\n- **Monitoring & Testing:** Regular re-identification testing and\n  continuous policy updates based on new threats or technologies.\n**In short:** Compliance requires **proven, well-governed anonymization\nand pseudonymization methods** applied consistently, monitored\ncontinuously, and validated against re-identification risks.\n---",
  "PR-6": "To comply with **PR-6 (\"How do you monitor and prevent privacy\nviolations during system operation?\")**, organizations must show that\nthey **actively detect, prevent, and respond** to privacy breaches\nthrough continuous monitoring and governance mechanisms.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Procedures:** Privacy Monitoring and Incident Management\n  Policies outlining monitoring, detection, and escalation protocols for\n  privacy breaches.\n- **Monitoring Tools & Technology:** Implementation of **Data Loss\n  Prevention (DLP)**, **AI monitoring**, and **logging tools** (e.g.,\n  access logs, transaction logs) that track and alert on suspicious\n  activity or unauthorized access.\n- **Access Control:** RBAC and access segmentation ensuring only\n  authorized personnel can access sensitive data, with regular access\n  reviews.\n- **Incident Response & Mitigation:** Formal response plan defining\n  containment, notification, and remediation processes; breach logs\n  documenting actions taken.\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Active involvement of a **Privacy\n  Governance Committee** or **Data Protection Officer (DPO)** reviewing\n  reports and ensuring accountability.\n- **Testing & Audits:** Regular validation of monitoring systems and\n  penetration tests to confirm detection capability.\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Lessons learned reports, feedback loops,\n  and system updates based on incident reviews or regulatory changes.\n**In short:** Compliance requires **proactive monitoring, strict access\ncontrol, automated alerts, and a clear response process** to prevent and\nmitigate operational privacy violations.\n---",
  "PR-7": "To comply with **PR-7 (\"Are privacy risks evaluated during both the\ndevelopment and deployment phases?\")**, organizations must show a\n**lifecycle-based approach** to identifying and mitigating privacy risks\nfrom design to real-world operation.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Procedures:** A **Privacy by Design Policy** integrating\n  privacy into every stage of the AI lifecycle; documented risk\n  assessment procedures for both development and deployment.\n- **Privacy Risk Assessments:** **Privacy Impact Assessments (PIAs)**\n  and, where applicable, **Data Protection Impact Assessments (DPIAs)**\n  evaluating data flows, design decisions, and operational risks.\n- **Design & Development Practices:** Evidence of privacy-focused design\n  artifacts (e.g., data flow diagrams, threat models) and adherence to\n  secure coding standards emphasizing encryption, access controls, and\n  data minimization.\n- **Deployment Safeguards:** Testing and monitoring in live environments\n  to detect data leakage or unauthorized access, with mitigation plans\n  in place.\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Reviews and approvals from governance\n  committees or the **Data Protection Officer (DPO)**, with audit trails\n  documenting identified risks and resolutions.\n- **Training & Continuous Improvement:** Developer and operator training\n  on privacy risk mitigation; lessons-learned reviews feeding into\n  process updates.\n**In short:** Compliance requires **proactive, documented, and ongoing\nprivacy risk evaluation** throughout AI system development and\ndeployment.\n---",
  "PR-8": "To comply with **PR-8 (\"What steps are taken to ensure compliance with\ncross-border data protection laws?\")**, organizations must demonstrate\nthat all **international data transfers** follow lawful mechanisms and\nmaintain equivalent privacy safeguards across jurisdictions.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Procedures:** A **Data Transfer Policy** defining legal\n  bases, safeguards, and jurisdictions for data movement; updated\n  **Privacy Policy** informing users about cross-border transfers.\n- **Legal Agreements:** Use of **Standard Contractual Clauses (SCCs)**,\n  **Binding Corporate Rules (BCRs)**, and **Data Processing Agreements\n  (DPAs)** ensuring partner and vendor compliance.\n- **Risk Assessments:** Completion of **Transfer Impact Assessments\n  (TIAs)** and **Data Protection Impact Assessments (DPIAs)** evaluating\n  destination-country risks and documenting mitigations.\n- **Technical Safeguards:** Encryption, anonymization, and\n  pseudonymization applied to all data in transit and at rest across\n  borders.\n- **Governance & Oversight:** DPO review and, where applicable, a\n  **Cross-Border Data Governance Committee** approving or monitoring\n  transfers.\n- **Compliance Monitoring:** Tools auditing data flows and maintaining\n  records of regulatory submissions or adequacy decisions.\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Regular policy reviews following\n  regulatory changes, audits, or incidents.\n**In short:** Compliance requires **clear legal frameworks, technical\nsafeguards, documented risk assessments, and active oversight** to\nprotect data across multiple jurisdictions.\n---",
  "RE-1": "To comply with **RE-1 (\"How do you test the AI system to ensure\nconsistent performance under different conditions?\")**, organizations\nmust show that **systematic, repeatable testing is performed across\nvaried scenarios** to verify stability, accuracy, and robustness.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Testing Policies & Frameworks:** A documented *AI Testing Policy*\n  defining performance benchmarks, stress testing standards, and test\n  coverage (normal, edge, and failure conditions).\n- **Test Plans & Cases:** Comprehensive test documentation, including\n  automated test scripts, scenario matrices, and success criteria.\n- **Tools & Technologies:** Use of model evaluation and performance\n  tools (*MLflow*, *TensorFlow Model Analysis*, *JMeter*, *Locust*) and\n  simulated environments to test real-world variability.\n- **Performance Metrics:** Reports tracking accuracy, latency, recall,\n  and system resource usage during tests.\n- **Stress & Edge Testing:** Evidence of high-load or boundary-condition\n  testing to expose weaknesses.\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Clear ownership (QA engineers, validators)\n  with sign-offs, reviews, and issue tracking logs.\n- **Continuous Testing:** Regression testing after updates, version\n  control logs, and ongoing monitoring for drift.\n- **Compliance:** Alignment with ISO/IEC, NIST, and industry-specific\n  reliability standards.\n**In short:** Compliance requires **structured, evidence-based\nreliability testing** that validates consistent AI performance under\ndiverse operational conditions and data variations.\n---",
  "RE-2": "To comply with **RE-2 (\"Have you conducted stress tests or simulations\nto evaluate the system's robustness?\")**, organizations must prove that\ntheir AI systems are **systematically tested under extreme and adverse\nconditions** to identify weaknesses and confirm resilience.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Stress Testing Policy:** A documented framework defining test\n  objectives, scenarios (e.g., high data loads, hardware failures), and\n  success criteria for robustness.\n- **Test Plans & Simulations:** Detailed plans and scenarios replicating\n  extreme operational conditions, such as data spikes, network outages,\n  or adversarial inputs.\n- **Execution Evidence:** Test logs, performance metrics (e.g., latency,\n  throughput), and simulation results showing system behaviour under\n  stress.\n- **Tools & Platforms:** Use of testing tools like *Apache JMeter*,\n  *Locust*, *Chaos Monkey*, or *Gremlin*, and monitoring systems to\n  track performance degradation.\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Clear roles, stakeholder reviews, and\n  sign-offs validating test results.\n- **Resilience & Mitigation:** Records of system improvements (e.g.,\n  redundancy, autoscaling, failover mechanisms) derived from stress test\n  findings.\n- **Continuous Testing:** Evidence of periodic or event-triggered stress\n  tests and ongoing monitoring for degradation.\n- **Compliance:** Alignment with NIST, ISO 27001, and ethical testing\n  standards.\n**In short:** Compliance requires **formalized, repeatable stress\ntesting and simulation processes** that validate the AI system's ability\nto remain stable and resilient under pressure.\n---",
  "RE-3": "To comply with **RE-3 (\"What mechanisms are in place to monitor the\nsystem for issues like model drift or degradation?\")**, organizations\nmust demonstrate **continuous, automated monitoring** to detect and\naddress performance decay or data drift in AI systems.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Monitoring Policy:** Formal documentation defining *model drift*,\n  detection thresholds, and response protocols for degradation events.\n- **Monitoring Tools:** Use of automated platforms like *Evidently AI*,\n  *Fiddler*, *Databricks ML Monitoring*, or in-house dashboards tracking\n  KPIs such as accuracy, recall, and latency.\n- **Baseline Metrics & Thresholds:** Established reference performance\n  levels with alert triggers for deviations beyond acceptable limits.\n- **Drift Detection Processes:** Regular data and concept drift\n  analyses, supported by statistical tests or retraining triggers when\n  significant variance is detected.\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Assigned monitoring roles (data\n  scientists, ML engineers), review boards, and approval workflows for\n  corrective actions.\n- **Incident Management:** Logs of detected drift events, root-cause\n  analysis, retraining records, and fallback mechanisms (e.g., backup\n  models).\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Documentation of lessons learned, tool\n  enhancements, and updated policies based on past incidents.\n- **Regulatory Compliance:** Alignment with ISO/IEC, NIST, and GDPR\n  accountability standards.\n**In short:** Compliance requires **automated, policy-driven AI\nperformance monitoring** with clear thresholds, alerts, and governance\nto prevent unnoticed degradation.\n---",
  "RE-4": "To comply with **RE-4 (\"How do you define success metrics for the\nreliability of your AI system?\")**, organizations must demonstrate that\n**reliability metrics are clearly defined, measurable, aligned with\nobjectives, and regularly monitored**.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Metric Definition Policy:** A documented process for identifying,\n  approving, and maintaining reliability metrics aligned with\n  organizational goals, ethics, and regulatory obligations.\n- **Reliability Metrics Framework:** A standardized method ensuring\n  consistent, comparable definitions of success across systems and\n  projects.\n- **Defined Metrics:** Clear KPIs such as accuracy, precision, recall,\n  uptime, latency, error rate, fault tolerance, and drift thresholds.\n- **Benchmarking:** Baseline performance metrics and comparisons against\n  industry standards or historical performance.\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Assigned roles for defining, reviewing,\n  and validating metrics, with formal approval and periodic review\n  cycles.\n- **Monitoring Tools:** Dashboards and observability platforms (e.g.,\n  *Datadog*, *MLflow*, *Power BI*) used to track reliability metrics in\n  real-time.\n- **Alerts & Incident Management:** Threshold triggers, deviation\n  alerts, and documented remediation actions.\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Evidence of metric refinement, lessons\n  learned, and policy updates based on evolving needs or feedback.\n- **Compliance:** Demonstrated alignment with ISO/IEC, NIST, and ethical\n  reliability standards.\n**In short:** Compliance requires **structured, transparent, and\ncontinuously reviewed success metrics** to ensure consistent AI\nreliability and accountability.\n---",
  "RE-5": "To comply with **RE-5 (\"Have you identified potential failure points in\nthe system?\")**, organizations must prove they **proactively identify,\ndocument, and mitigate weaknesses** that could compromise AI reliability\nor safety.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Failure Point Identification Policy:** Formal procedures describing\n  how potential failure points are identified, prioritized, and\n  reviewed.\n- **Risk Assessments:** Comprehensive system risk analyses (e.g.,\n  *Failure Mode and Effects Analysis -- FMEA*) covering data pipelines,\n  model logic, dependencies, infrastructure, and interfaces.\n- **Testing & Simulation:** Evidence of stress, edge-case, and\n  fault-injection testing revealing vulnerabilities under adverse\n  conditions.\n- **Failure Point Register:** A maintained inventory listing each\n  failure point, its cause, likelihood, impact, and mitigation measures.\n- **Monitoring & Reporting:** Use of observability platforms (e.g.,\n  *Datadog*, *New Relic*) to detect anomalies, supported by incident and\n  root-cause logs.\n- **Mitigation & Resilience Strategies:** Documented safeguards such as\n  redundancy, failover, auto-scaling, or manual intervention workflows.\n- **Governance & Accountability:** Assigned roles for risk management,\n  regular oversight reviews, and post-mortem reports.\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Lessons learned and framework updates\n  based on emerging risks or past incidents.\n- **Regulatory Alignment:** Conformance with ISO/IEC, NIST, and ethical\n  reliability standards.\n**In short:** Compliance means **systematically mapping and mitigating\npotential AI failure points** through structured risk analysis,\nmonitoring, and continual improvement.\n---",
  "RE-6": "To comply with **RE-6 (\"How do you ensure the system performs\nconsistently under varying conditions (e.g., edge cases)?\")**,\norganizations must prove they **anticipate, test, and monitor AI\nperformance** across both normal and extreme operating conditions.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Consistency & Edge Case Policy:** A documented approach defining\n  edge cases, outlining testing strategies, and integrating them into\n  the organization's AI risk framework.\n- **Testing Frameworks:** Comprehensive test plans covering diverse and\n  adversarial scenarios (e.g., noisy inputs, high-load environments, or\n  unusual data distributions).\n- **Simulation Environments:** Use of virtual testbeds and synthetic\n  datasets to replicate rare or extreme events (e.g., *AnyLogic*,\n  *SimPy*, *CleverHans*, *ART*).\n- **Performance Validation:** Logs and reports demonstrating consistent\n  accuracy, latency, and reliability under tested variations.\n- **Monitoring & Analytics Tools:** Real-time observability systems\n  detecting inconsistencies and triggering alerts during live operation.\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Designated testing leads, stakeholder\n  reviews, and approval workflows for validation outcomes.\n- **Incident & Recovery Plans:** Documented mitigation strategies for\n  failures caused by edge conditions.\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Lessons learned, periodic framework\n  updates, and benchmarking against industry standards.\n**In short:** Compliance requires **structured edge case identification,\nrigorous testing, and proactive monitoring** to ensure AI reliability\nacross all possible operating conditions.\n---",
  "RE-7": "To comply with **RE-7 (\"Are there mechanisms for detecting and\naddressing system downtime or errors?\")**, organizations must show they\nhave **real-time detection, alerting, and rapid recovery processes** to\nmaintain AI system reliability and availability.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Incident Detection & Response Policies:** Formal procedures defining\n  how downtime or errors are identified, escalated, and resolved,\n  including defined alert thresholds.\n- **Monitoring Tools:** Use of APM and observability platforms (e.g.,\n  *Datadog*, *New Relic*, *Prometheus*, *WhyLabs*) to track uptime,\n  performance, and error rates in real time.\n- **Alerting Systems:** Automated alerts for anomalies, outages, or\n  degradation events.\n- **Error Logs & Downtime Reports:** Centralized log management (e.g.,\n  *Splunk*, *ELK Stack*) with documented root-cause analyses and\n  corrective actions.\n- **Redundancy & Failover:** Tested failover systems, backup servers,\n  and load-balancing configurations to reduce single points of failure.\n- **Incident Management:** Response logs, post-incident reviews, and\n  lessons-learned documentation.\n- **Preventive Maintenance:** Evidence of patching, monitoring, and\n  proactive risk mitigation activities.\n- **SLAs & Compliance:** Defined uptime targets, regulatory alignment\n  (ISO/IEC 27001), and accountability through governance review.\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Ongoing updates to tools and response\n  frameworks based on incident history.\n**In short:** Compliance requires **structured monitoring, automated\ndetection, and rapid response mechanisms** to minimize downtime and\nmaintain AI system resilience.\n---",
  "RE-8": "To comply with **RE-8 (\"How often is the AI model tested and retrained\nto maintain reliability?\")**, organizations must demonstrate that they\n**regularly test, retrain, and document model updates** to ensure\nsustained accuracy and relevance over time.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Model Maintenance Policy:** Formal documentation outlining\n  retraining frequency, performance thresholds, drift detection\n  triggers, and lifecycle reliability requirements.\n- **Testing & Retraining Framework:** Defined methodologies for testing,\n  validation, and retraining using approved tools and workflows.\n- **Retraining Schedule:** A documented plan specifying regular testing\n  intervals (e.g., monthly/quarterly) and event-driven retraining\n  triggers (e.g., performance drops, data drift).\n- **Performance Monitoring:** Continuous tracking of model metrics\n  (accuracy, F1-score, latency) with alerts for deviations.\n- **Retraining Records:** Logs of retraining events, version histories,\n  dataset updates, and post-retraining test outcomes.\n- **Governance Oversight:** Evidence of review and approval of\n  retraining schedules and validation results.\n- **Tools:** Automated platforms for retraining and monitoring (e.g.,\n  *MLflow*, *Kubeflow*, *Neptune.ai*).\n- **Risk & Compliance:** Risk assessments, fallback mechanisms, and\n  adherence to ISO/NIST standards.\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Lessons learned, policy refinements, and\n  stakeholder feedback integration.\n**In short:** Compliance requires a **structured, documented, and\nautomated retraining process** ensuring AI models remain accurate,\nstable, and aligned with changing data and operational environments.\n---",
  "SA-1": "To comply with **SA-1 (\"Have you conducted a safety impact assessment\nfor the AI system?\")**, organizations must prove they **formally assess,\ndocument, and mitigate potential harms** their AI systems could cause to\npeople, operations, or the environment.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Procedures:** A **Safety Assessment Policy** and **Risk\n  Management Policy** mandating Safety Impact Assessments (SIAs) before\n  deployment, with guidance on identifying and addressing safety risks.\n- **SIA Reports:** Comprehensive records showing identified risks,\n  likelihood and severity ratings, and implemented mitigations (e.g.,\n  fail-safes, redundancy, shutdown mechanisms).\n- **Risk Identification & Mitigation:** Use of structured methods (risk\n  matrices, FMEA, HACCP) to prioritize and mitigate potential failures\n  or unsafe behaviours.\n- **Testing & Validation:** Evidence of pre-deployment and simulation\n  testing to verify safety performance and effectiveness of mitigations.\n- **Standards Alignment:** Demonstrated compliance with **ISO/IEC 23894\n  (AI Safety)**, **ISO 31000**, and **NIST AI RMF**.\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Review by a **safety or AI governance\n  committee** and periodic safety audits.\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Lessons learned from incidents and policy\n  updates.\n**In short:** Compliance requires a **structured, auditable SIA\nprocess** ensuring AI systems are designed, tested, and monitored to\nminimize harm and meet recognized safety standards.\n---",
  "SA-2": "To comply with **SA-2 (\"How do you ensure that the system does not cause\nharm in unintended ways?\")**, organizations must show a **proactive,\nend-to-end strategy** for identifying, testing, and mitigating\nunintentional harm from AI systems.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Procedures:** An **AI Safety Policy** and **Risk\n  Management Policy** defining how unintended harm is detected,\n  prevented, and managed throughout the system lifecycle.\n- **Risk Assessment:** Documentation of **hazard identification**,\n  **FMEA/FTA analyses**, and **Safety Impact Assessments (SIAs)**\n  covering risks such as bias, misinterpretation, or malfunction.\n- **Testing & Validation:** Results from **scenario testing**, **edge\n  case analysis**, and **fail-safe validation** demonstrating safe\n  system behaviour under stress or anomalies.\n- **System Design Controls:** Inclusion of **human-in-the-loop\n  oversight**, **fail-safe mechanisms**, and **Explainable AI (XAI)**\n  features enabling detection of unintended outcomes.\n- **Continuous Monitoring:** Tools and logs tracking anomalies, false\n  positives/negatives, and user feedback for real-time harm prevention.\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Safety review committees, incident\n  response frameworks, and periodic audits ensuring accountability.\n- **Training & Improvement:** Ongoing staff education and policy updates\n  based on incidents or lessons learned.\n**In short:** Compliance requires **robust policies, risk-based testing,\nmonitoring, and human oversight** to detect and prevent AI from causing\nharm unintentionally.\n---",
  "SA-3": "To comply with **SA-3 (\"Are there monitoring tools in place to detect\nunsafe system behaviour in real-time?\")**, organizations must\ndemonstrate **active, automated monitoring and response capabilities**\nthat can detect, alert, and mitigate unsafe AI behaviour as it happens.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Procedures:** A **Real-Time Monitoring Policy** and\n  **Incident Response Policy** defining how unsafe behaviour is\n  detected, logged, and escalated.\n- **Monitoring Tools & Technology:** Implementation of **anomaly\n  detection**, **real-time logging**, and **alerting platforms** (e.g.,\n  Splunk, DataDog, Prometheus) integrated with SIEM or ticketing systems\n  (ServiceNow, Jira).\n- **Anomaly Detection & Fail-Safes:** Predefined thresholds, automated\n  shutdowns, and **AI-specific drift detection** tools that identify\n  deviations in inputs, outputs, or behaviour.\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Regular audits, oversight committees, and\n  documented root-cause analyses for detected issues.\n- **Testing & Validation:** Simulation of unsafe scenarios and\n  validation of tool performance and response speed.\n- **Training & Metrics:** Trained operators with clear monitoring\n  guidelines; KPIs measuring detection rate and response time.\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Lessons-learned reviews and tool\n  enhancements following incidents or near-misses.\n**In short:** Compliance requires **real-time visibility, automated\nanomaly detection, and coordinated incident response** to promptly\ndetect and neutralize unsafe AI behaviour.\n---",
  "SA-4": "To comply with **SA-4 (\"How do you handle situations where the system's\nactions conflict with human safety requirements?\")**, organizations must\nshow that **human safety always overrides system autonomy** through\nrobust policies, fail-safes, and governance.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Procedures:** A **Safety Policy** and **Risk Management\n  Policy** defining escalation paths and decision-making when AI actions\n  conflict with safety rules.\n- **Fail-Safe Mechanisms:** Evidence of **automated shutdown\n  protocols**, **emergency stop systems**, and **manual override\n  controls** enabling human intervention at any time.\n- **Incident Response Framework:** Plans and logs detailing how safety\n  conflicts are identified, escalated, investigated, and resolved,\n  including post-incident reviews.\n- **System Design & Testing:** Human-centric designs, **simulations of\n  conflict scenarios**, and validation reports confirming the AI system\n  prioritizes safety over performance.\n- **Monitoring & Detection:** Real-time tools and alerts identifying\n  potential conflicts between AI behaviour and safety requirements.\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Safety review boards, audits, and\n  compliance with **ISO/IEC 23894** (AI Safety) and **ISO 31000** (Risk\n  Management).\n- **Training & Improvement:** Staff training on override use and\n  incident handling, plus lessons learned driving policy and system\n  updates.\n**In short:** Compliance means **humans stay in control**---AI must\nstop, alert, or defer whenever safety is at risk.\n---",
  "SA-5": "To comply with **SA-5 (\"Are there emergency protocols for shutting down\nthe system in case of unsafe behaviour?\")**, organizations must\ndemonstrate **tested, documented, and effective shutdown procedures**\nthat protect users and systems during unsafe events.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Procedures:** A formal **Emergency Shutdown Policy** and\n  **Incident Response Plan** defining when and how shutdowns occur, who\n  authorizes them, and post-shutdown actions.\n- **Shutdown Mechanisms:** Presence of **automated shutdown features**\n  (e.g., anomaly-triggered halts) and **manual override controls**\n  allowing human intervention. Redundant systems should support safe\n  degradation or recovery.\n- **Testing & Validation:** Logs and reports of **simulation and stress\n  testing**, verifying both automatic and manual shutdown effectiveness\n  under various failure scenarios.\n- **Monitoring & Detection:** Tools that continuously detect unsafe\n  behaviour and trigger alerts or automated shutdowns when thresholds\n  are breached.\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Reviews and audits by safety committees\n  confirming the protocols' reliability and compliance with **ISO/IEC\n  23894** and **ISO 31000** standards.\n- **Training & Documentation:** Operator training records, **emergency\n  playbooks**, and post-incident analyses demonstrating preparedness and\n  learning.\n**In short:** Compliance requires a **proven ability to detect danger,\nhalt operations safely, and recover gracefully** through well-defined,\nregularly tested shutdown protocols.\n---",
  "SA-6": "To comply with **SA-6 (\"What steps have you taken to identify and\nmitigate potential risks or harm caused by the AI system?\")**,\norganizations must demonstrate a **structured, proactive risk management\nprocess** that identifies, assesses, and mitigates harms across the AI\nlifecycle.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Procedures:** A formal **Risk Identification and\n  Mitigation Policy** and **Ethical AI Policy** defining\n  responsibilities, review cycles, and harm prevention principles.\n- **Risk Assessments:** Documented **risk analyses**, **Safety Impact\n  Assessments (SIAs)**, and **threat modeling** covering bias,\n  unintended outcomes, and system vulnerabilities.\n- **Mitigation Strategies:** Implementation of **risk mitigation\n  plans**, algorithm refinements, dataset bias reduction, and\n  **technical safeguards** such as input validation and anomaly\n  detection.\n- **Testing & Monitoring:** Simulation, stress testing, and **model\n  validation reports** confirming stability and reliability, supported\n  by **real-time monitoring tools** and incident logs.\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Review by **risk oversight committees**,\n  supported by audits ensuring alignment with **ISO/IEC 23894**, **ISO\n  31000**, and **NIST AI RMF**.\n- **Training & Improvement:** Staff training, lessons learned, and\n  regular policy updates to strengthen future risk management.\n**In short:** Compliance requires **end-to-end governance, testing, and\ncontinuous refinement** to minimize potential harm and ensure the AI\nsystem operates safely and ethically.\n---",
  "SA-7": "To comply with **SA-7 (\"Are there fail-safe mechanisms or redundancies\nin place to address unexpected system behaviours?\")**, organizations\nmust prove that **their AI systems can detect, respond to, and recover\nsafely** from unexpected failures or anomalies.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Procedures:** A **Fail-Safe Policy** and **Incident\n  Response Policy** defining activation conditions, responsibilities,\n  and integration with broader safety frameworks.\n- **System Design:** Documentation of **fail-safe mechanisms** (e.g.,\n  automated shutdowns, rollback to safe states, component isolation) and\n  **redundancy systems** (backup servers, redundant storage, alternate\n  data pathways). Evidence of **graceful degradation** to maintain\n  limited functionality under stress.\n- **Testing & Validation:** Logs from **simulation, stress, and\n  redundancy tests** confirming mechanisms activate as expected and\n  handoffs occur seamlessly.\n- **Monitoring & Detection:** Use of **real-time anomaly detection\n  tools** and **incident logs** showing historical fail-safe\n  activations.\n- **Governance & Compliance:** Oversight by safety committees and\n  alignment with **ISO/IEC 23894 (AI Safety)** and **ISO 31000 (Risk\n  Management)**.\n- **Training & Playbooks:** Staff trained to execute manual fail-safes,\n  supported by **operational playbooks** for rapid response.\n**In short:** Compliance demonstrates that the system is **resilient,\nrecoverable, and capable of protecting people and assets** when failures\noccur unexpectedly.\n---",
  "SA-8": "To comply with **SA-8 (\"Have you conducted safety testing in realistic\nor high-risk scenarios?\")**, organizations must demonstrate that their\nAI systems have been **rigorously tested under real-world and extreme\nconditions** to ensure reliability and safety.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Procedures:** A formal **Safety Testing Policy** defining\n  objectives, methodologies, and reporting; and integration of testing\n  into the overall **Risk Management Framework**.\n- **Test Planning & Documentation:** Comprehensive **test plans** and\n  **scenario design documents** showing realistic, edge-case, and\n  high-risk conditions---such as data anomalies, environmental\n  stressors, and adversarial attacks.\n- **Testing & Validation Results:** Detailed **safety test reports**,\n  **simulation results**, and **validation findings** confirming that\n  the AI performs safely under all tested conditions.\n- **Monitoring & Incident Management:** Real-time test monitoring logs,\n  **incident records**, and **root cause analyses** from detected issues\n  during testing.\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Review and approval by **testing oversight\n  committees** or external auditors, demonstrating adherence to\n  **ISO/IEC 23894 (AI Safety)** and **ISO/IEC 29119 (Software\n  Testing)**.\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Documentation of updates, lessons learned,\n  and design adjustments informed by test results.\n**In short:** Compliance requires **proving the AI system remains safe\nand stable even in high-risk, unpredictable conditions** through\nstructured, evidence-based safety testing.\n---",
  "SE-1": "To comply with **SE-1 (\"What measures are in place to protect your AI\nmodels and data from unauthorized access or tampering?\")**,\norganizations must prove they have **comprehensive technical,\nprocedural, and governance controls** safeguarding AI assets.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **AI Security & Data Protection Policies:** Formal documentation\n  covering encryption, access control, and incident response for AI\n  models and datasets.\n- **Access Controls:** Role-based access control (RBAC), principle of\n  least privilege (PoLP), MFA, and detailed authorization logs.\n- **Encryption Standards:** AES-256 for data at rest, TLS 1.2+ for data\n  in transit, and secure key management via HSM or cloud key vaults.\n- **Network & Infrastructure Security:** Firewalls, IDS/IPS, zero-trust\n  architecture, and hardened containerized deployments (e.g.,\n  Docker/Kubernetes).\n- **Model Integrity Controls:** Model hashing, tamper-evident\n  repositories, versioning, and runtime integrity monitoring.\n- **Monitoring & Alerts:** Centralized logging (e.g., Splunk, LogRhythm)\n  and anomaly detection for suspicious access or data manipulation.\n- **Regular Assessments:** Vulnerability scanning, penetration testing,\n  and third-party audits of AI systems.\n- **Incident Response & Recovery:** Defined procedures for containment,\n  remediation, and secure backup recovery.\n- **Compliance & Training:** Alignment with ISO 27001/SOC 2 and ongoing\n  staff security awareness.\n**In short:** Compliance requires **layered security, continuous\nmonitoring, and proactive governance** to prevent unauthorized access or\ntampering with AI models and data.\n---",
  "SE-2": "To comply with **SE-2 (\"How do you handle adversarial risks, such as\nattacks designed to manipulate the AI's outputs?\")**, organizations must\nshow they have **policies, testing, and mitigation mechanisms** to\nprevent and respond to adversarial manipulation of AI models and data.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Adversarial Risk Policy:** Formal documentation defining adversarial\n  threats (e.g., data poisoning, evasion), assessment methods, and\n  response plans.\n- **Threat Modelling:** Reports analyzing vulnerabilities and likely\n  attack vectors, supported by simulated adversarial attack tests.\n- **Model Hardening:** Defensive measures such as adversarial training,\n  input validation, gradient masking, and regularization to reduce\n  susceptibility.\n- **Monitoring & Detection:** Automated anomaly detection systems to\n  flag sudden performance drops, unusual input patterns, or tampering\n  attempts.\n- **Access Controls:** RBAC, API authentication, and rate limiting to\n  restrict potential attack surfaces.\n- **Data Protection:** Input filtering, dataset audits, and encryption\n  of model parameters to prevent poisoning or manipulation.\n- **Incident Response:** Dedicated adversarial incident response plans\n  with defined containment, forensics, and retraining steps.\n- **Compliance & Governance:** Alignment with standards (e.g., NIST\n  Adversarial ML RMF, ISO/IEC AI Security) and regular oversight\n  reviews.\n- **Training & Awareness:** Staff education on identifying and\n  mitigating adversarial risks.\n**In short:** Compliance requires **proactive threat modelling, robust\ndefensive design, and ongoing monitoring** to protect AI systems from\nadversarial manipulation.\n---",
  "SE-3": "To comply with **SE-3 (\"Do you conduct regular security audits or\npenetration testing on your AI systems?\")**, organizations must\ndemonstrate a **structured, repeatable, and well-documented process**\nfor assessing AI security vulnerabilities and remediating findings.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Security Audit & Penetration Testing Policies:** Formal policies\n  defining testing frequency, scope (AI models, APIs, infrastructure),\n  and procedures for addressing findings.\n- **Audit & Testing Records:** Reports documenting vulnerabilities\n  discovered, remediation actions taken, and adherence to standards\n  (e.g., ISO/IEC 27001, NIST AI RMF).\n- **Scope & Methodology:** Defined scope covering models, data\n  pipelines, and IT systems, using frameworks such as *OWASP Top 10 for\n  AI* or *MITRE ATT&CK for AI*.\n- **Tools & Technology:** Use of automated scanners and specialized AI\n  testing tools (e.g., Adversarial Robustness Toolbox, Burp Suite,\n  Nessus).\n- **Governance Oversight:** Roles assigned for conducting, reviewing,\n  and validating audit outcomes; oversight committee reviews and\n  approvals.\n- **Incident & Risk Management:** Documentation of remediation logs,\n  incident reports, and post-test reviews.\n- **Third-Party Assessments:** Independent audits or penetration tests\n  conducted by external AI security specialists.\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Lessons learned and updates to testing\n  procedures based on evolving threats.\n**In short:** Compliance requires **regular AI-focused audits and\npenetration tests** with verified follow-up, governance oversight, and\ncontinuous vulnerability management.\n---",
  "SE-4": "To comply with **SE-4 (\"How do you secure the AI model during training,\ndeployment, and operation?\")**, organizations must show evidence of\n**end-to-end security controls** that protect the AI model and its data\nacross all lifecycle stages --- training, deployment, and operation.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Procedures:** AI security and data protection policies\n  defining controls for each lifecycle phase, with assigned roles and\n  responsibilities.\n- **Secure Training Practices:** Use of isolated training environments\n  (e.g., Docker, Kubernetes), RBAC, activity logging, and secure\n  preprocessing or anonymization of training data.\n- **Secure Deployment:** Hardened environments, version control (e.g.,\n  DVC, GitHub Enterprise), encrypted APIs with OAuth and TLS, and\n  network segmentation via firewalls or IDS/IPS.\n- **Operational Security:** Real-time monitoring, tamper detection\n  (e.g., cryptographic hashing), and comprehensive access and change\n  logs.\n- **Access Control:** RBAC, MFA, and periodic privileged-access reviews\n  to limit exposure.\n- **Integrity & Resilience:** Model integrity verification, encrypted\n  backups, and tested recovery processes.\n- **Vulnerability & Patch Management:** Regular vulnerability scans,\n  dependency updates, and patch tracking.\n- **Governance & Incident Response:** Oversight committee reviews,\n  incident response plans, and post-incident improvement records.\n**In short:** Compliance demands **secure-by-design AI lifecycle\nmanagement** --- protecting the model, data, and environments through\nencryption, isolation, monitoring, and continuous governance.\n---",
  "SE-5": "To comply with **SE-5 (\"Are there safeguards to prevent unauthorized\nmodifications to the AI model?\")**, organizations must demonstrate\n**strong access control, integrity verification, and change management**\nmechanisms that protect AI models from tampering or unapproved\nalterations.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Procedures:** AI model security and change management\n  policies defining approval workflows, monitoring, and incident\n  response for unauthorized modifications.\n- **Access Controls:** Role-based access control (RBAC), least-privilege\n  permissions, MFA, and secure API authentication to restrict model\n  interaction.\n- **Logging & Monitoring:** Continuous logging of all access and\n  modification attempts, supported by anomaly detection and automated\n  alerts for suspicious activity.\n- **Integrity Verification:** Cryptographic hashing, checksums, and\n  tamper-evident mechanisms (e.g., blockchain tracking) to verify model\n  authenticity.\n- **Secure Development & Deployment:** Use of secure repositories (e.g.,\n  GitHub Enterprise) with commit approvals, branch protections, and\n  CI/CD pipelines that include automated security checks.\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Required approvals before model changes,\n  routine audits of modification records, and governance committee\n  reviews.\n- **Incident Response:** Plans detailing detection, containment, and\n  recovery procedures following unauthorized changes.\n- **Compliance & Training:** Alignment with NIST AI RMF, ISO/IEC 27001,\n  and ongoing staff education on secure model management.\n**In short:** Compliance requires **robust technical and procedural\nsafeguards** ensuring only authorized, traceable, and verified\nmodifications to AI models.\n---",
  "SE-6": "To comply with **SE-6 (\"What measures are in place to protect the system\nagainst adversarial attacks such as input manipulation?\")**,\norganizations must demonstrate **comprehensive defences** against\nadversarial threats that aim to deceive or manipulate AI models.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Procedures:** Adversarial risk mitigation and AI security\n  policies detailing how input manipulation and related threats are\n  identified, tested, and managed.\n- **Threat Modeling & Risk Assessment:** Documentation identifying\n  adversarial risks (e.g., data poisoning, evasion, inversion) and their\n  potential impact.\n- **Defensive Techniques:** Evidence of adversarial training, robust\n  input validation, regularization, and noise resistance techniques to\n  strengthen model resilience.\n- **Monitoring & Detection:** Use of anomaly detection, behavioural\n  analytics, and alert systems to identify suspicious inputs or outputs.\n- **Secure Deployment:** Enforced API security (rate limiting,\n  authentication, input validation) and model version control to prevent\n  exploitation.\n- **Tools & Frameworks:** Use of testing tools like *Adversarial\n  Robustness Toolbox* or *CleverHans*, and AI security platforms such as\n  *CalypsoAI* or *Robust Intelligence*.\n- **Incident Response:** Plans and logs documenting adversarial attack\n  handling, root cause analysis, and recovery measures.\n- **Governance & Training:** Defined responsibilities, regular audits,\n  and staff education on adversarial risks.\n**In short:** Compliance requires **proactive adversarial risk\nmanagement** through testing, monitoring, policy control, and continuous\nresilience improvement.\n---",
  "SE-7": "To comply with **SE-7 (\"How do you protect sensitive data in your AI\npipelines?\")**, organizations must show evidence of **robust data\nprotection mechanisms**---from collection to disposal---covering\nencryption, tokenization, access control, and governance.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Procedures:** Data protection and AI pipeline security\n  policies specifying encryption, tokenization, anonymization, and roles\n  responsible for data safeguarding.\n- **Encryption Practices:** Use of AES-256 encryption for data at rest\n  and TLS 1.2+ for data in transit, plus secure key management (e.g.,\n  AWS KMS, Azure Key Vault) with rotation and RBAC access.\n- **Tokenization & Anonymization:** Implementation of tokenization and\n  privacy-enhancing techniques such as differential privacy, hashing, or\n  suppression of identifiers.\n- **Access Controls:** RBAC enforcement and least-privilege access for\n  data handling across all pipeline stages.\n- **Secure Pipeline Design:** Segmentation of sensitive data, secure VPC\n  or container environments, and continuous monitoring for anomalies.\n- **Data Masking & Synthetic Data:** Dynamic masking during development\n  and synthetic data use to prevent unnecessary exposure.\n- **Compliance & Governance:** Alignment with GDPR, Australian Privacy\n  Act 1988, and ISO 27001, supported by governance reviews and data\n  classification frameworks.\n- **Incident Response:** Data-breach plans and post-incident improvement\n  records.\n**In short:** Compliance requires **end-to-end data protection**\nensuring encryption, minimization, and controlled handling of sensitive\ndata throughout the AI lifecycle.\n---",
  "SE-8": "To comply with **SE-8 (\"Are there regular vulnerability assessments and\npenetration tests performed on the AI system?\")**, organizations must\nshow that they **routinely identify, test, and remediate** security\nweaknesses across all AI components.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Procedures:** Documented vulnerability management and\n  penetration testing policies defining frequency, scope, methodologies\n  (e.g., OWASP AI, MITRE ATLAS), and remediation workflows.\n- **Testing Records:** Completed vulnerability and penetration test\n  reports outlining findings, severity, remediation actions, and\n  resolution timelines.\n- **Scope & Methodology:** Evidence that testing covers AI models,\n  datasets, APIs, infrastructure, and deployment pipelines using static\n  and dynamic analysis.\n- **Tools & Frameworks:** Use of scanning tools (e.g., Nessus, Qualys,\n  OpenVAS) and AI-specific tools (e.g., Adversarial Robustness Toolbox,\n  CleverHans, Burp Suite, Metasploit).\n- **Governance:** Scheduled testing cadence, assigned responsibilities,\n  and audit logs ensuring tests occur consistently and findings are\n  tracked.\n- **Remediation & Risk Management:** Logs showing timely resolution and\n  inclusion of vulnerabilities in broader risk registers.\n- **Third-Party Validation:** Independent external penetration tests and\n  certifications (e.g., ISO 27001, SOC 2) verifying internal practices.\n- **Continuous Improvement:** Lessons learned, updated methodologies,\n  and training on emerging AI attack vectors.\n**In short:** Compliance requires **structured, recurring security\ntesting** with proven remediation, oversight, and independent validation\nof AI system resilience.\n---",
  "SU-1": "To comply with **SU-1 (\"What is the environmental impact of training and\ndeploying your AI system?\")**, organizations must **measure, monitor,\nand minimize** the environmental footprint of their AI lifecycle ---\nparticularly energy use and carbon emissions.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Commitments:** A clear **Sustainability Policy**\n  outlining goals for reducing AI-related energy consumption and\n  emissions.\n- **Impact Assessments:** **Energy usage and carbon footprint reports**\n  (using tools like *CodeCarbon* or *Green Algorithms*), plus\n  **lifecycle assessments** covering development to decommissioning.\n- **Metrics & Monitoring:** Benchmark data on energy intensity,\n  computational efficiency, and emission trends, tracked through\n  internal dashboards or cloud provider tools.\n- **Optimization Strategies:** Documentation showing **model and\n  infrastructure efficiency improvements**, such as model pruning,\n  workload scheduling, or use of low-power hardware.\n- **Renewable Energy & Offsetting:** Evidence of **green cloud\n  partnerships** (e.g., AWS or Google Cloud sustainability programs) and\n  **carbon offset initiatives** like reforestation or verified credit\n  schemes.\n- **Governance & Standards:** Oversight by a **sustainability\n  committee**, environmental audits, and compliance with standards such\n  as **ISO 14001** or **PAS 2060**.\n**In short:** Compliance demonstrates a measurable, transparent effort\nto **reduce AI's environmental impact** through efficiency, renewable\nenergy, and responsible lifecycle management.\n---",
  "SU-2": "To comply with **SU-2 (\"Have you explored opportunities to optimize\nalgorithms for greater computational efficiency?\")**, organizations must\nshow they **actively reduce the computational and energy demands** of\ntheir AI systems through algorithmic and infrastructure optimization.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Commitments:** A formal **Efficiency Optimization\n  Policy** defining goals for minimizing computational load and energy\n  use across AI lifecycles.\n- **Algorithm Design & Development:** Use of **lightweight models**\n  (e.g., MobileNet, DistilBERT) and **techniques such as pruning,\n  quantization, and knowledge distillation** to cut unnecessary\n  parameters while maintaining accuracy.\n- **Tools & Frameworks:** Implementation of **optimization toolkits**\n  like *TensorFlow Model Optimization Toolkit*, *PyTorch Profiler*, or\n  *ONNX* for efficient model deployment.\n- **Metrics & Validation:** Reports comparing **resource consumption\n  before and after optimization**, with proof that accuracy and\n  reliability remain stable.\n- **Monitoring & Continuous Improvement:** Ongoing tracking of **GPU/CPU\n  utilization, energy use, and latency**, leading to iterative\n  refinements.\n- **Infrastructure Efficiency:** Adoption of **energy-efficient hardware\n  (TPUs, optimized GPUs)** and **cloud auto-scaling features** to\n  prevent idle resource waste.\n- **Governance & Standards:** Oversight through efficiency review\n  committees and adherence to **ISO 14001** and energy-efficient AI\n  design principles.\n**In short:** Compliance demonstrates a deliberate, measurable effort to\n**optimize algorithms for sustainability, cost-effectiveness, and\nperformance efficiency**.\n---",
  "SU-3": "To comply with **SU-3 (\"Are you using cloud services or infrastructure\nthat prioritizes renewable energy sources?\")**, organizations must show\nthat **cloud and infrastructure choices intentionally align with\nsustainability goals** by prioritizing renewable energy--powered\nproviders.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Commitments:** A formal **Sustainability Policy**\n  committing to renewable energy use in infrastructure and cloud\n  decisions.\n- **Cloud Provider Selection:** Evidence that selected providers (e.g.,\n  *Google Cloud*, *AWS*, *Microsoft Azure*) have **carbon-neutral or\n  renewable energy certifications** such as *RECs*, *CarbonNeutral®*, or\n  *Green-e*.\n- **Energy Source Documentation:** Records verifying renewable power\n  usage in data centres or vendor contracts explicitly mandating green\n  energy sourcing.\n- **Metrics & Monitoring:** Regular **energy usage and sustainability\n  reports** tracking renewable energy proportions and comparing\n  performance to industry benchmarks.\n- **Optimization Practices:** Proof of **energy-efficient deployment\n  strategies**---like auto-scaling, region selection based on renewable\n  availability, and the use of cloud tools (e.g., *Google Cloud's Carbon\n  Footprint* or *AWS Customer Carbon Tool*).\n- **Governance & Compliance:** Oversight via **sustainability\n  committees** and alignment with **ISO 14001** or **ISO 50001** energy\n  management standards.\n**In short:** Compliance demonstrates a proactive, measurable approach\nto **reducing carbon impact** by selecting and operating within cloud\nenvironments powered by renewable energy.\n---",
  "SU-4": "To comply with **SU-4 (\"What is the estimated carbon footprint of\ntraining and deploying your AI system?\")**, organizations must\n**quantify, monitor, and mitigate greenhouse gas emissions** associated\nwith AI training, inference, and operation.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Commitments:** A formal **Sustainability or Carbon\n  Management Policy** with measurable goals for reducing AI-related\n  emissions.\n- **Carbon Assessment Reports:** Detailed **calculations of energy\n  consumption and CO₂ equivalent emissions**, using tools such as\n  *CodeCarbon*, *Green Algorithms*, or *Google Cloud's Carbon\n  Footprint*.\n- **Metrics & Monitoring:** Tracking **energy usage (kWh)**, grid\n  emission factors, and benchmarking results against internal or\n  industry standards.\n- **Optimization Strategies:** Evidence of **energy-efficient model\n  design** (e.g., pruning, quantization, or lightweight architectures)\n  and **green infrastructure use** (e.g., renewable-powered data\n  centres).\n- **Offsets & Mitigation:** Participation in **carbon offset programs**,\n  renewable energy investments, or reforestation projects to compensate\n  for emissions.\n- **Governance & Compliance:** Oversight by **sustainability\n  committees**, audits validating emission data, and alignment with\n  standards such as **ISO 14064** and the **GHG Protocol**.\n**In short:** Compliance demonstrates an evidence-based effort to\n**measure, reduce, and offset AI-related carbon emissions**, ensuring\nresponsible environmental stewardship throughout the AI lifecycle.\n---",
  "SU-5": "To comply with **SU-5 (\"Have you optimized your algorithms to reduce\ncomputational resource requirements?\")**, organizations must show that\nthey've **systematically improved algorithmic efficiency** to minimize\nhardware, energy, and cost demands while preserving performance.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Commitments:** A formal **Efficiency Optimization\n  Policy** defining measurable goals for reducing compute and energy\n  usage.\n- **Algorithmic Optimization:** Proof of applying techniques such as\n  **model pruning**, **quantization**, **knowledge distillation**, and\n  use of **lightweight architectures** (e.g., *MobileNet*,\n  *DistilBERT*).\n- **Tools & Frameworks:** Use of optimization toolkits like **TensorFlow\n  Model Optimization Toolkit**, **PyTorch Quantization Toolkit**, or\n  **ONNX Runtime**, plus profiling tools (e.g., *TensorBoard*, *NVIDIA\n  Nsight*).\n- **Metrics & Validation:** Reports showing **reductions in memory,\n  GPU/CPU load, and inference latency**, with trade-off analyses\n  confirming accuracy retention.\n- **Deployment & Monitoring:** Evidence of **resource-efficient\n  deployments** (e.g., serverless, containerized, batched inference) and\n  continuous monitoring of compute performance.\n- **Governance & Compliance:** Oversight through internal review\n  committees and alignment with **energy-efficient AI best practices**\n  and **ISO 14001** sustainability principles.\n**In short:** Compliance demonstrates a structured, data-driven approach\nto **algorithm optimization**, achieving measurable efficiency gains\nthat reduce environmental impact and operational costs without degrading\nmodel quality.\n---",
  "SU-6": "To comply with **SU-6 (\"Are you using hardware or cloud platforms that\nprioritize energy efficiency?\")**, organizations must demonstrate that\ntheir **AI infrastructure is intentionally chosen and managed for\nminimal energy consumption and environmental impact**.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Commitments:** A formal **Energy Efficiency Policy**\n  linking hardware and infrastructure decisions to sustainability\n  objectives.\n- **Energy-Efficient Hardware:** Proof of using **energy-optimized GPUs,\n  CPUs, or TPUs**, ideally with **ENERGY STAR** or equivalent\n  certifications, and documentation of workload optimization or cooling\n  efficiency.\n- **Cloud Platform Selection:** Evidence that cloud providers hold **ISO\n  50001**, **CarbonNeutral®**, or similar certifications, and publish\n  sustainability reports confirming energy-efficient data-center\n  operations.\n- **Metrics & Monitoring:** Regularly tracked **energy-usage data and\n  efficiency benchmarks** for hardware and cloud resources.\n- **Optimization & Renewable Integration:** Documentation of **load\n  balancing, auto-scaling**, or **virtualization strategies**, and\n  confirmation that data centres use or offset **renewable energy**.\n- **Governance & Compliance:** Oversight via sustainability committees,\n  periodic energy audits, and adherence to **ISO 50001** or **ASHRAE**\n  efficiency standards.\n**In short:** Compliance shows a measurable, policy-driven commitment to\n**reducing the energy footprint of AI operations** through efficient\nhardware choices, renewable-powered cloud infrastructure, and continuous\nperformance monitoring.\n---",
  "SU-7": "To comply with **SU-7 (\"How do you balance sustainability goals with\nperformance requirements?\")**, organizations must demonstrate a\n**structured, evidence-based approach** to managing trade-offs between\n**energy efficiency** and **system performance** throughout the AI\nlifecycle.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Commitments:** A formal **Sustainability and Performance\n  Policy** setting clear goals for both efficiency and output quality.\n- **Trade-Off Frameworks:** Documentation of **structured decision\n  frameworks or matrices** evaluating sustainability versus performance\n  impacts during design and deployment.\n- **Algorithm Optimization:** Proof that techniques like **model\n  pruning, quantization, or knowledge distillation** are used to lower\n  computational demand without degrading accuracy.\n- **Metrics & Monitoring:** Dual tracking of **energy consumption,\n  carbon footprint, inference speed, and accuracy**, with real-time\n  dashboards and historical comparisons.\n- **Infrastructure Efficiency:** Use of **energy-optimized hardware\n  (e.g., TPUs)** and **cloud features** such as auto-scaling or\n  energy-aware regions to maintain balance.\n- **Governance & Review:** Oversight by **cross-functional committees**\n  ensuring sustainability is embedded in performance decisions,\n  supported by audits and periodic reviews.\n**In short:** Compliance demonstrates that the organization deliberately\nmanages **sustainability-performance trade-offs**, ensuring high system\neffectiveness with minimized environmental impact through measurable,\ntransparent, and continuously improved practices.\n---",
  "SU-8": "To comply with **SU-8 (\"Do you track the environmental impact of your AI\nsystems over time?\")**, organizations must show a **sustained,\ndata-driven approach** to monitoring and improving the environmental\nperformance of their AI operations.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Commitments:** A documented **Environmental Tracking\n  Policy** outlining goals for long-term impact monitoring and footprint\n  reduction.\n- **Metrics & Monitoring:** Continuous tracking of **energy usage,\n  carbon emissions, and data center efficiency (e.g., PUE)** using tools\n  like *Google Cloud Carbon Footprint*, *AWS Sustainability Dashboard*,\n  or *CodeCarbon*.\n- **Data Collection & Analysis:** Historical datasets showing **trends\n  and progress** in emissions reduction, including project-level energy\n  tracking.\n- **Reporting & Transparency:** Regular **environmental reports**\n  aligned with standards such as the **GHG Protocol** or **ISO 14064**,\n  and stakeholder disclosures showing accountability.\n- **Optimization & Improvement:** Documentation showing that tracked\n  data informs **algorithm, hardware, or workflow optimizations** to\n  reduce impact.\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Evidence of **sustainability committees**,\n  **audits**, and adherence to **ISO 50001 / ISO 14001** standards.\n**In short:** Compliance demonstrates that the organization **actively\nmeasures, reports, and improves** the environmental footprint of its AI\nsystems, ensuring continuous alignment with sustainability goals and\nrecognized environmental standards.",
  "TR-1": "To comply with **TR-1 (\"What level of detail about the AI system's\nfunctionality and limitations is communicated to users or\nstakeholders?\")**, organizations should demonstrate **clear, accessible,\nand audience-appropriate transparency** around what their AI can and\ncannot do.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Standards:** A *Transparency Policy* and *Communication\n  Guidelines* defining what information must be shared with users and\n  stakeholders, including known limitations and risks.\n- **Documentation:** Detailed technical documentation for internal\n  audiences and simplified user-facing materials (manuals, FAQs,\n  visuals) that explain AI functionality, boundaries, and potential\n  biases.\n- **Explainability & Tools:** Use of interpretability tools (*SHAP*,\n  *LIME*) or interactive dashboards that help visualize AI decision\n  factors and limitations.\n- **Stakeholder Engagement:** Records of workshops, feedback sessions,\n  or updates showing that stakeholders were informed about system\n  performance and constraints.\n- **Testing & Validation:** Usability tests proving users understood\n  system capabilities and limitations, with results used to improve\n  clarity.\n- **Compliance:** Evidence that disclosures align with legal or ethical\n  standards (e.g., GDPR \"right to explanation\", ISO/IEC AI standards).\n**In short:** Compliance requires proactively communicating the AI\nsystem's purpose, strengths, and limitations in clear, audience-relevant\nlanguage to foster transparency, trust, and accountability.\n---",
  "TR-2": "To comply with **TR-2 (\"How do you document the data sources,\nalgorithms, and processes used in your AI system?\")**, organizations\nshould maintain **comprehensive, version-controlled documentation**\ncovering all key components of their AI systems.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Standards:** A *Documentation Policy* defining required\n  detail for data sources, algorithms, and processes, and *Transparency\n  Standards* guiding documentation depth for different audiences.\n- **Data Records:** Metadata describing data origin, collection methods,\n  preprocessing steps, and demographic representativeness, with dataset\n  version histories and audit logs.\n- **Algorithm Documentation:** Clear explanations of algorithm purpose,\n  logic, and parameters; flowcharts illustrating decision pathways; and\n  version histories with testing results and risk assessments.\n- **Process Documentation:** Records of lifecycle stages (design →\n  testing → deployment), key decision logs, and workflow descriptions\n  showing how AI integrates into business operations.\n- **Tools & Governance:** Use of platforms like *Git*, *Confluence*, or\n  *Jupyter Notebooks* for documentation; oversight from governance\n  bodies and audit trails verifying compliance.\n- **Accessibility & Updates:** Centralized storage, accessible to\n  relevant teams, with change logs and continuous improvement records.\n**In short:** Compliance requires structured, auditable documentation\nthat enables traceability, accountability, and transparency across all\nAI data, algorithm, and process elements.\n---",
  "TR-3": "To comply with **TR-3 (\"Are there mechanisms for external parties to\nunderstand how your AI system works?\")**, organizations must show that\n**regulators, customers, and other stakeholders have clear, structured\naccess** to information explaining their AI system's purpose, design,\nand limitations.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Standards:** A *Transparency Policy* and *Stakeholder\n  Communication Guidelines* defining what information is shared\n  externally, how often, and through what channels.\n- **External-Facing Documentation:** User guides, FAQs, or *Transparency\n  Reports* explaining the AI system's data use, logic, and limitations\n  in plain language.\n- **Access Mechanisms:** Public dashboards, secure portals, or APIs that\n  allow stakeholders to view summaries or explore decision logic through\n  interpretable visualizations (e.g., via *SHAP*, *LIME*).\n- **Governance & Validation:** Evidence that communication tools are\n  approved by oversight bodies, tested with regulators or customers for\n  clarity, and updated based on feedback.\n- **Third-Party Assurance:** External audits or certifications\n  validating that transparency mechanisms meet industry or regulatory\n  standards (e.g., *GDPR*, *ISO/IEC 23053*).\n**In short:** Compliance requires user-friendly, verifiable mechanisms\nfor external parties to understand and trust your AI system's\nfunctionality, promoting accountability and ethical transparency.\n---",
  "TR-4": "To comply with **TR-4 (\"How is information about your AI system shared\nwith internal teams and external stakeholders?\")**, organizations must\nshow **structured, audience-appropriate communication processes**\nensuring everyone understands how the AI system works.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Standards:** An *Information Sharing Policy* and\n  *Transparency Guidelines* defining what information is shared, with\n  whom, and through which channels.\n- **Internal Communication:** Centralized documentation (e.g.,\n  *Confluence*, *SharePoint*), training materials, and cross-functional\n  meetings to align technical, compliance, and business teams.\n- **External Communication:** Tailored materials for\n  stakeholders---technical reports for regulators, non-technical FAQs or\n  infographics for customers, and dashboards showing system metrics and\n  transparency updates.\n- **Governance & Access Control:** Approval records from governance\n  committees, audit logs confirming compliant sharing, and measures\n  ensuring sensitive information is securely shared.\n- **Feedback & Engagement:** Evidence of stakeholder engagement\n  sessions, feedback loops, and change logs showing updates following\n  system improvements or stakeholder input.\n- **Compliance:** Documentation proving adherence to relevant\n  transparency and data regulations (*GDPR*, *ISO/IEC 23053*).\n**In short:** Compliance requires effective, secure, and well-governed\ninformation sharing that keeps both internal teams and external\nstakeholders informed, aligned, and confident in the AI system's\ntransparency.\n---",
  "TR-5": "To comply with **TR-5 (\"Are there any 'black-box' components in your\nsystem that could impede transparency?\")**, organizations must identify\nopaque AI components and demonstrate **active measures to explain,\nmonitor, and mitigate their impact** on transparency.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Standards:** A *Transparency Policy* and *Explainability\n  Standards* defining how black-box components are identified,\n  evaluated, and managed.\n- **System Documentation:** Architecture diagrams and component\n  assessments highlighting any opaque models (e.g., deep learning\n  networks) and explaining associated transparency risks.\n- **Mitigation Measures:** Use of explainability tools (*SHAP*, *LIME*,\n  counterfactual analysis), surrogate interpretable models, or human\n  oversight processes to validate AI decisions.\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Records from review committees approving\n  black-box model use, with documented justifications, decision logs,\n  and transparency risk assessments.\n- **Monitoring & Reporting:** Transparency dashboards, incident logs,\n  and regular audits tracking interpretability levels and corrective\n  actions.\n- **Training & Communication:** Staff training on explainability\n  techniques and stakeholder communications disclosing black-box\n  limitations and mitigation strategies.\n**In short:** Compliance requires recognizing where black-box models\nexist, transparently managing their risks, and demonstrating a\nstructured approach to making complex AI systems understandable and\naccountable.\n---",
  "TR-6": "To comply with **TR-6 (\"How do you disclose the sources of data and\nalgorithms used in the AI system?\")**, organizations must demonstrate\n**structured, transparent disclosure practices** that explain where data\noriginates and how algorithms are developed or sourced.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Standards:** A *Disclosure Policy* and *Transparency\n  Guidelines* defining what information must be shared, the level of\n  detail required, and how frequently it's updated.\n- **Data Source Disclosure:** Comprehensive records of all data sources\n  (internal, third-party, open datasets), including origin, licensing,\n  preprocessing steps, and identified limitations or biases.\n- **Algorithm Disclosure:** Documentation describing algorithm purpose,\n  functionality, parameters, and risks---plus visual flowcharts or\n  explainability outputs (e.g., *SHAP*, *LIME*) for clarity.\n- **Communication & Accessibility:** Regularly published *Transparency\n  Reports*, stakeholder-specific summaries, or interactive dashboards\n  giving access to data and algorithm details.\n- **Governance & Oversight:** Approval logs from governance committees,\n  audit records confirming accuracy, and stakeholder feedback\n  mechanisms.\n- **Compliance & Training:** Evidence of adherence to *GDPR Articles\n  13--14*, *ISO/IEC 23053*, and internal staff training on disclosure\n  requirements.\n**In short:** Compliance requires clear, verifiable, and accessible\ndisclosures about data and algorithms---ensuring transparency,\naccountability, and trust with all stakeholders.\n---",
  "TR-7": "To comply with **TR-7 (\"Are users made aware when they are interacting\nwith an AI system?\")**, organizations must show that **users are\nclearly, consistently, and accessibly informed** when engaging with\nAI-driven systems.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Standards:** A *Transparency Policy* and *User Awareness\n  Standards* detailing how and when AI interaction disclosures are made.\n- **Notification Mechanisms:** Visible, upfront labels (\"AI Assistant,\"\n  \"Powered by AI\"), pop-ups, or banners in interfaces; statements in\n  *Terms of Service* or *Privacy Policies*; and icons or badges denoting\n  AI involvement.\n- **User Education:** FAQs, onboarding messages, or guides explaining AI\n  roles and limitations, translated and accessible across languages and\n  formats.\n- **Governance & Testing:** Approval from internal governance or legal\n  teams, audit logs confirming consistent implementation, and usability\n  testing verifying that users understand AI disclosures.\n- **Accessibility & Compliance:** Adherence to *WCAG accessibility\n  standards* and legal requirements such as *GDPR Article 22* (automated\n  decision-making transparency).\n- **Feedback & Continuous Improvement:** Systems for collecting user\n  feedback, incident logs for missed disclosures, and updates ensuring\n  ongoing compliance.\n**In short:** Compliance requires explicit, accessible, and well-tested\nnotifications ensuring users always know when they're interacting with\nAI---supporting transparency, accountability, and user trust.\n---",
  "TR-8": "To comply with **TR-8 (\"Do you publish any transparency reports or\ndocumentation for regulators or customers?\")**, organizations must\ndemonstrate a **consistent, structured approach to publishing AI\ntransparency reports** that communicate system performance, risks, and\ngovernance to stakeholders.\n**Key evidence types:**\n- **Policies & Standards:** A *Transparency Reporting Policy* and\n  *Publishing Standards* defining content scope, frequency (e.g.,\n  annual/quarterly), and review procedures for reports.\n- **Transparency Reports:** Published documents detailing the AI\n  system's purpose, data sources, algorithms, performance metrics,\n  risks, mitigation steps, and incident handling. Reports may be\n  tailored for different audiences---technical for regulators,\n  plain-language summaries for customers.\n- **Accessibility & Governance:** Reports made available via public\n  websites or dashboards, reviewed and approved by governance or legal\n  teams, and redacted to protect proprietary information.\n- **Monitoring & Updates:** Logs showing regular updates following\n  system changes, audit reports validating accuracy, and compliance with\n  *GDPR* or *ISO/IEC 23053* standards.\n- **Engagement & Feedback:** Evidence of stakeholder communication,\n  feedback loops, and improvements made based on external input.\n**In short:** Compliance requires regularly published, accessible\ntransparency reports that demonstrate accountability, build trust, and\nprovide clear visibility into AI systems for both regulators and\ncustomers.\n---"
};
